WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:05.644
PETER: Why did you
pack it up from California?

00:00:05.644 --> 00:00:11.110
Why'd you move from San Francisco
when you're in tech, to Kyiv?

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.716
JOHN: Straight off the bat,
not a little foreplay or anything first?

00:00:13.716 --> 00:00:15.649
-Zero foreplay on this.
-Okay.

00:00:16.606 --> 00:00:19.216
-So what was it? Were you missing...

00:00:19.216 --> 00:00:21.300
Was something not right at home?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.609
What was it?

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:24.908
Like, why would you just get up and go?

00:00:24.908 --> 00:00:26.493
JOHN: It's a lot of reasons.

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:30.353
The biggest one that initially sparked me
is just knowing a Ukrainian.

00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:32.485
I was living in a hacker hideout.

00:00:32.485 --> 00:00:35.854
That sort of ghetto hacker house and...

00:00:35.854 --> 00:00:37.882
PETER: Ghetto hacker house
in San Francisco?

00:00:37.882 --> 00:00:40.062
-Yeah.
-What do you mean ghetto in San Francisco?

00:00:40.062 --> 00:00:42.922
-I mean, like, a girl came over
and she's like,

00:00:42.922 --> 00:00:45.275
"There's a guy
smoking heroin in the doorway."

00:00:45.275 --> 00:00:48.148
and I'm like,
"Oh, don't worry about him." [laughs]

00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:51.213
-So this is obviously
the rough part of San Francisco.

00:00:51.213 --> 00:00:53.551
Which there is
and most people have no clue about.

00:00:53.551 --> 00:00:55.617
-Exactly, people who live in San Francisco

00:00:55.617 --> 00:00:57.284
sometimes don't know this place exists.

00:00:57.284 --> 00:00:57.873
-Right.

00:00:57.897 --> 00:00:59.375
So you met a Ukrainian in there?

00:00:59.375 --> 00:01:02.203
-Yes, I met Andre the first night.

00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:07.608
He's out on the street
right there when I walk in

00:01:07.608 --> 00:01:10.381
-and he says...
-He wasn't doing crack or anything?

00:01:10.381 --> 00:01:12.511
-LSD.
-LSD, seriously?

00:01:12.511 --> 00:01:13.512
-Eyeballs are huge.

00:01:13.512 --> 00:01:16.687
He's like, "Do you want to go
burn Christmas trees on the beach?

00:01:16.687 --> 00:01:19.859
PETER: So this was your first
introduction to Ukraine, through Andre?

00:01:19.859 --> 00:01:22.228
-Yeah.
-This guy with bugged-out LSD eyes?

00:01:22.228 --> 00:01:23.353
-He's a f*cking genius.

00:01:23.353 --> 00:01:24.958
Like, later that night

00:01:24.958 --> 00:01:27.282
we're hanging out in the common room,
seven or eight of us

00:01:27.282 --> 00:01:30.321
and he has his desktop set up there
and he's typing so fast

00:01:30.321 --> 00:01:31.437
and we're laughing, joking

00:01:31.437 --> 00:01:32.743
and suddenly the typing stops.

00:01:32.743 --> 00:01:34.817
So we all look over at Andre.

00:01:35.364 --> 00:01:36.907
He's like, "I fixed a bug."

00:01:36.907 --> 00:01:38.447
And we're like, "Yeah!"

00:01:38.447 --> 00:01:42.182
-You came from San Diego
which is warm, sunny, nice beaches.

00:01:43.208 --> 00:01:44.208
So...

00:01:44.339 --> 00:01:49.361
Okay, there's startup issues
in San Francisco but...

00:01:50.482 --> 00:01:54.724
What else would spark you
to make such a drastic move?

00:01:54.724 --> 00:01:56.642
You'd never even been
to this part of the world before.

00:01:56.642 --> 00:01:58.469
-One, Maidan was really cool.

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:01.782
I was living with Andre
when Maidan was happening

00:02:01.782 --> 00:02:03.530
and that was totally new for me.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:04.530
And...

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:09.666
Seeing people in the streets
with like shields, and helmets, and fire

00:02:10.049 --> 00:02:13.319
that's something that we don't
ever have in America, like at all.

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:16.104
-1865 was the last time.

00:02:16.104 --> 00:02:17.883
-Right?
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:17.883 --> 00:02:19.628
-Over a hundred years.
-Civil war.

00:02:19.628 --> 00:02:23.917
JOHN: And so I actually admired
the Ukrainians because it sounded like

00:02:23.917 --> 00:02:25.126
they were essentially betrayed

00:02:25.126 --> 00:02:27.185
and they were pissed off,
and they were standing up for themselves.

00:02:27.185 --> 00:02:28.018
-Right.

00:02:28.018 --> 00:02:31.292
-And to do those kinds of things
you have to be brave.

00:02:31.316 --> 00:02:34.514
You have to care about yourself
and about your country

00:02:34.514 --> 00:02:36.990
and those people
are kind of like heroes to me.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.912
And so on another level

00:02:38.912 --> 00:02:42.020
I thought that Ukrainians
are just cool people.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.128
Like, I admired them

00:02:43.128 --> 00:02:45.738
and so that's another thing
that triggered me to go there.

00:02:45.738 --> 00:02:48.645
Another one is just knowing Andre
and liking him as a person

00:02:48.645 --> 00:02:53.445
and he has kind of some of the esoterical
and hippie vibes as well that I like.

00:02:53.445 --> 00:02:56.246
If he's from there then there would
be other people like that from here

00:02:56.246 --> 00:02:59.708
and so I knew it would be likely that I'd
enjoy people and have friends and stuff.

00:02:59.708 --> 00:03:00.549
-Okay.

00:03:00.573 --> 00:03:02.642
-Another reason is girls.

00:03:02.642 --> 00:03:04.432
Just liking women.

00:03:04.432 --> 00:03:08.001
That's something that's
automatically gonna be attractive here.

00:03:08.001 --> 00:03:09.390
It's definitely a factor

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.232
but it's hard to separate
everything out together.

00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:14.034
Like 100%, it wasn't what drove me here.

00:03:14.034 --> 00:03:16.634
When you're adding all the calculations

00:03:17.001 --> 00:03:19.320
they just added up to a level where

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.510
despite having a good life back home
and not needing to make a move

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.100
and all of the risk
and possible discomfort

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.144
the calculation just seemed
kind of easy and I have a big goal

00:03:28.144 --> 00:03:29.934
and I'm pretty ambitious and I know that

00:03:29.934 --> 00:03:32.685
in order to do those things
I have to push my comfort zone

00:03:32.685 --> 00:03:34.470
and I have to play my cards right.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.314
PETER: To me it doesn't sound crazy
but I think

00:03:36.314 --> 00:03:41.674
to a lot of Ukrainians or Americans
it's pretty drastic.

00:03:41.674 --> 00:03:45.903
-Well then you backed it up
because even though it made sense

00:03:45.903 --> 00:03:48.018
you had been in Ukraine.

00:03:48.018 --> 00:03:49.089
You loved the country

00:03:49.089 --> 00:03:50.582
-you spoke highly of it.
-Yeah.

00:03:50.582 --> 00:03:54.413
-In our friend group we had some Ukrainian,
Russian, Slavic people that I liked

00:03:54.413 --> 00:03:56.187
and they just seemed like cool people.

00:03:56.187 --> 00:04:01.259
They're tough, they're smart,
they're just capable.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:07.581
PETER: You didn't feel any nervousness
of leaving come, coming to here?

00:04:07.581 --> 00:04:12.171
There was no apprehension or...
[chuckles]

00:04:12.171 --> 00:04:14.429
Like, walk me through it.

00:04:14.429 --> 00:04:15.652
-I had a return ticket.

00:04:15.652 --> 00:04:18.728
Okay, so coming to here...

00:04:21.164 --> 00:04:26.914
Right, the day I'm about to leave
I find out that the Turkish government is

00:04:26.914 --> 00:04:31.012
being overthrown, or having a revolution,
and I'm flying through Istanbul.

00:04:31.525 --> 00:04:33.191
-Summer 2016?
-Yeah.

00:04:33.191 --> 00:04:35.242
So if you believe in signs or anything.

00:04:35.242 --> 00:04:36.789
I don’t' really but...

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:39.372
[laughing] it just seemed like a bad time.

00:04:39.372 --> 00:04:40.719
[John inhales deeply]

00:04:40.719 --> 00:04:43.908
-That was the year
the airport was bombed out too.

00:04:43.908 --> 00:04:46.452
-Yeah, it was literally
right after the airport got bombed

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.374
that I was supposed to be there
and I'm like...

00:04:48.374 --> 00:04:51.274
-Oh well.
-Then the revolution is kicking off.

00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:53.918
So you came here.

00:04:53.918 --> 00:04:56.750
You landed in Boryspil, the airport.

00:04:57.636 --> 00:04:59.160
What were the thoughts?

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:00.710
What were the emotions
running through your head?

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:04.021
Were you like,
"Hell yeah, I'm on to this new challenge."

00:05:04.021 --> 00:05:06.541
or were you like,
"Oh sh*t, what am I doing here?"

00:05:06.541 --> 00:05:08.141
or what was it like?

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.833
-I was really happy to see free Wi-Fi.

00:05:10.833 --> 00:05:12.044
That's, like, really cool.

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:13.784
-Free Wi-Fi in the airport?
-Yeah.

00:05:13.784 --> 00:05:16.819
-So many places don't have free Wi-Fi.
-Right, right.

00:05:16.819 --> 00:05:17.854
True.

00:05:17.854 --> 00:05:18.974
-Got in a taxi.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.031
Driver's asking for 500 hryvnias,
whatever that is

00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:26.284
and I double checked by cross-referencing
another taxi for the ride

00:05:26.284 --> 00:05:27.350
and they also said 500.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:29.454
So I'm like,
"Cool, I know I'm not being ripped off."

00:05:29.454 --> 00:05:32.293
And it turned out
I was being ripped off but...

00:05:32.293 --> 00:05:34.610
-So you got into the city center.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.171
Did you have somewhere to go
or where were you going?

00:05:36.171 --> 00:05:39.253
-So Andre's company
is taking off a lot

00:05:39.253 --> 00:05:42.766
and he has an employee
who was gonna host me.

00:05:42.766 --> 00:05:47.549
And so I got dropped off at her place
and it was a pretty warm welcome.

00:05:47.549 --> 00:05:49.137
Very cool person.

00:05:49.137 --> 00:05:51.050
An attractive female programmer
is something

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.828
that's not very common
in American as well.

00:05:52.828 --> 00:05:55.810
Which is just like
a sort of nice landing here.

00:05:56.524 --> 00:05:59.058
It's not always the case
that you go to a new country

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.924
and like, everybody's
down to just help you.

00:06:02.516 --> 00:06:03.647
-Right, right, right.

00:06:03.647 --> 00:06:06.247
-Uh, and that was just really welcoming.

00:06:06.271 --> 00:06:09.333
-Do you think it's what you
put out to the world is what you get back?

00:06:09.333 --> 00:06:13.572
Or you think, honestly, here,
people just are open and give more?

00:06:13.572 --> 00:06:14.653
-I think it's both.

00:06:14.653 --> 00:06:18.079
I think people here
don't count time like in America.

00:06:18.079 --> 00:06:20.050
-Right, right, right.
-In America...

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:21.866
There's a little bit more stress

00:06:21.866 --> 00:06:24.597
especially San Francisco
'cause things are moving so fast.

00:06:24.597 --> 00:06:27.210
It's like if you're not growing,
you're dying.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.977
-I think there's like
overall in the US

00:06:28.977 --> 00:06:32.066
there's an anxiety
in the culture of like...

00:06:33.123 --> 00:06:36.016
It's this life deferment
mentality of like, you gotta,

00:06:36.016 --> 00:06:37.897
"In five years, I'll be doing this...."

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:40.258
"and retirement time,
I'll be doing this." right?

00:06:40.258 --> 00:06:42.941
-And that doesn't exist here at all.
-Time is money over there.

00:06:42.941 --> 00:06:44.880
-Every second is counted for, yeah.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.508
-Yeah, it's not counted.
People are more patient.

00:06:47.508 --> 00:06:50.902
They enjoy conversations for
the sake of conversations a little more.

00:06:50.902 --> 00:06:53.910
It seems like people have
less ulterior motives.

00:06:54.226 --> 00:06:56.718
It's overall just more relaxing

00:06:56.718 --> 00:07:00.387
and I've noticed myself,
by being here I've become more relaxed

00:07:00.387 --> 00:07:04.227
and it just seems like
a more genuine real way to live life.

00:07:04.227 --> 00:07:07.026
On some levels, being here
compared to America

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:10.961
reminds me of being a child in some ways.

00:07:10.961 --> 00:07:14.984
In a good way
where time seems to stop a little bit more.

00:07:15.328 --> 00:07:18.417
I can go into an abandoned building
and, like, hang out.

00:07:18.417 --> 00:07:20.110
Laws seem a little bit more flexible

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.223
whereas in America
if I go in an abandoned building

00:07:22.223 --> 00:07:24.204
it's like a big deal and really bad.

00:07:24.204 --> 00:07:25.396
-Right, right, right.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:26.980
-The world's more
fresh and interesting

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.296
and here, every block
is its own unique adventure.

00:07:30.296 --> 00:07:32.847
In America all the blocks are...

00:07:32.847 --> 00:07:36.332
"Wow, there's, like, a McDonalds,
and a Subway, and a Macy's right here."

00:07:36.332 --> 00:07:39.802
I've seen a thousand of those before
and they’re all the same.

00:07:39.802 --> 00:07:43.095
In Ukraine there's
a lot more interesting stuff going on.

00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:44.885
-Like what else
was going through you?

00:07:44.885 --> 00:07:47.443
Were you like,
"Okay, I'm just gonna do a week here."?

00:07:47.443 --> 00:07:48.794
A year here?

00:07:48.794 --> 00:07:49.976
Like, what are you thinking?

00:07:49.976 --> 00:07:52.998
-So I think it was about two weeks,
two and a half weeks.

00:07:52.998 --> 00:07:55.695
I had a return ticket back and I was like,

00:07:55.695 --> 00:08:00.381
"All right, I'm going to
hire a team of developers."

00:08:00.757 --> 00:08:01.757
First step.

00:08:01.757 --> 00:08:04.270
And then figure it out from there.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:08.084
And at the same time
I wanted to explore the city

00:08:08.084 --> 00:08:10.113
and decide if this
is a place I'd wanna move.

00:08:10.113 --> 00:08:12.964
'Cause I knew that if I could move here
it'd be more effective.

00:08:12.964 --> 00:08:15.227
Being here, being able to
operate and talk to people.

00:08:15.227 --> 00:08:17.302
-So in a way you were
on a kinda scouting trip

00:08:17.302 --> 00:08:19.794
to see if you wanted to see
if you wanted to live here instead?

00:08:19.794 --> 00:08:21.757
-Yeah, work
and work-pleasure in a way.

00:08:21.757 --> 00:08:23.442
Like I was trying to enjoy the city

00:08:23.442 --> 00:08:25.862
but for the sake of knowing
if I could work here

00:08:25.862 --> 00:08:27.279
and I really liked it.

00:08:27.279 --> 00:08:29.037
There was so much raw stuff.

00:08:29.037 --> 00:08:31.624
I had got on CouchSurfing
before coming here...

00:08:31.624 --> 00:08:32.321
-Yeah.

00:08:32.321 --> 00:08:35.521
-And normally
a guy on CouchSurfing is like...

00:08:36.661 --> 00:08:38.535
"Hey, can I stay on your couch?"
"Hey, can I stay on your couch?"

00:08:38.535 --> 00:08:41.219
20, 30 people
and finally somebody says yes.

00:08:41.219 --> 00:08:44.721
Here, I made an advertisement...
or just some sort of post.

00:08:44.721 --> 00:08:45.355
-Right.

00:08:45.355 --> 00:08:48.832
-And about 30 people
reached out to offer to host me.

00:08:48.832 --> 00:08:50.328
Like 30 reached out to me.

00:08:50.328 --> 00:08:51.006
-Wow.

00:08:51.006 --> 00:08:54.685
-And that's like a thousand times
different from other places.

00:08:54.685 --> 00:08:55.576
-Dude, they're super...

00:08:55.576 --> 00:08:58.189
I find the Ukrainians
super open to foreigners.

00:08:58.213 --> 00:09:00.825
Like, they're very open minded to guests.
-Yes.

00:09:00.825 --> 00:09:02.714
-And people coming here for the most part.

00:09:02.714 --> 00:09:04.331
-And it made me feel welcome.

00:09:04.331 --> 00:09:06.119
-Yeah.
-Just these people who are interested...

00:09:06.119 --> 00:09:09.127
to hang out, and to talk,
and to show me around.

00:09:11.174 --> 00:09:15.267
PETER: You gotta be miserable to uproot...
Uproot your life completely

00:09:15.267 --> 00:09:17.296
and go to a different part of the world.

00:09:17.296 --> 00:09:18.416
Or what is it?

00:09:18.809 --> 00:09:21.332
I could answer this question too
but you go for it.

00:09:21.332 --> 00:09:22.703
-Um, I wasn’t sure

00:09:22.703 --> 00:09:26.460
but it became pretty clear
pretty quickly that this place just

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.993
seems so much more real and authentic.

00:09:29.686 --> 00:09:32.067
I felt like there's
a little piece of humanity

00:09:32.067 --> 00:09:35.791
that's missing in California
that I found here.

00:09:36.675 --> 00:09:39.829
I feel more alive here and...

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:43.029
It's not just that it's good for business.

00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:46.450
Like, even if GameTree's blowing up...

00:09:47.174 --> 00:09:49.242
-I meant that's...
-Blowing up in a good way.

00:09:49.242 --> 00:09:51.100
-Yeah, blowing up in a good way.

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.411
It's important for me,
like, that that's...

00:09:53.411 --> 00:09:55.573
I see that as a path to my ultimate goals

00:09:55.573 --> 00:09:58.701
but I'm gonna try to (buy us)
staying here however much possible.

00:09:58.701 --> 00:10:00.416
I've kind of fallen in love
with the country.

00:10:00.416 --> 00:10:01.426
I wanna promote the country.

00:10:01.426 --> 00:10:04.397
I helped with the Connect Ukraine
Festival. about showcasing

00:10:04.397 --> 00:10:06.692
some of the best things
that Ukraine has to offer to foreigners

00:10:06.692 --> 00:10:10.297
to invest in the country and get it
more on the international scene.

00:10:10.838 --> 00:10:15.030
JOHN: One of the biggest challenges is that
about half of the apartments were fake

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.417
-and they were real estatements fishing...
-Seriously?

00:10:17.417 --> 00:10:21.944
-To get people to visit
or to get people's contact information.

00:10:21.944 --> 00:10:24.574
Like literally about 50% of the listings
are just straight up, fake.

00:10:24.574 --> 00:10:27.382
Which I think should be
highly regulated and illegal

00:10:27.382 --> 00:10:28.485
and it's very frustrating

00:10:28.485 --> 00:10:30.744
'cause I've moved a few times now,
different apartments

00:10:30.744 --> 00:10:33.789
and every time
it's just such a pain in the ass.

00:10:33.789 --> 00:10:36.007
Getting excited,
seeing the pictures and the price

00:10:36.007 --> 00:10:37.502
and then realizing
that they're just fishing.

00:10:37.502 --> 00:10:39.636
Like that should not be allowed.

00:10:39.757 --> 00:10:41.648
PETER: So that's a big challenge.

00:10:41.648 --> 00:10:42.817
What else is a challenge?

00:10:42.817 --> 00:10:45.642
Or what were the big challenges
when you moved in?

00:10:45.642 --> 00:10:48.232
-So we lived together and you were like,

00:10:48.232 --> 00:10:50.801
"Juke, Ukraine's awesome, I'm coming in."

00:10:50.801 --> 00:10:52.473
"Save that extra room."

00:10:52.473 --> 00:10:54.250
and so I Airbnb'd it out
for a little bit...

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:54.929
-Yeah.

00:10:54.953 --> 00:10:58.257
-And there were those
digital nomads cruising though.

00:10:58.257 --> 00:11:01.176
They're blogging, traveling a lot.

00:11:01.546 --> 00:11:04.655
I thought they'd be a bit tough
from living that kind of lifestyle

00:11:04.655 --> 00:11:07.327
but the second that
the hot water cut out...

00:11:07.327 --> 00:11:08.032
-Right.

00:11:08.032 --> 00:11:09.427
-Which was totally unexpected.

00:11:09.427 --> 00:11:10.663
Never seen that in America

00:11:10.663 --> 00:11:14.031
but they were flipping out
without hot water.

00:11:14.031 --> 00:11:15.862
-Seriously?
-Yeah, like...

00:11:15.862 --> 00:11:18.313
A long time ago,
hot water wasn't even a thing.

00:11:18.313 --> 00:11:19.122
-Right.

00:11:19.122 --> 00:11:23.245
-And I take cold showers
just 'cause sometimes

00:11:23.245 --> 00:11:26.149
but for them, that was
like a major inconvenience.

00:11:26.149 --> 00:11:28.839
-That was in like the middle
of the summer too, right?

00:11:28.839 --> 00:11:30.359
-It was, yeah.

00:11:30.359 --> 00:11:33.467
And so that made me...

00:11:33.467 --> 00:11:35.439
It was one of the things I noticed
about the difference

00:11:35.439 --> 00:11:38.206
between Americans
and Ukrainians is that here

00:11:38.206 --> 00:11:41.060
you don't expect
perfect comfort all the time.

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:42.584
-Right, right, right.

00:11:42.584 --> 00:11:44.243
-Whereas they're Americans.

00:11:44.243 --> 00:11:46.552
Like, they kind of reacted...

00:11:46.552 --> 00:11:49.507
It was almost like...
I was almost embarrassed

00:11:49.507 --> 00:11:51.981
by how spoiled they were.

00:11:51.981 --> 00:11:54.591
That just some cold water
was such a big deal.

00:11:54.591 --> 00:11:58.105
-Right, like at the forefront of their day

00:11:58.105 --> 00:11:59.264
these creature comforts

00:11:59.264 --> 00:12:02.201
and then everything
comes in behind that or something, right?

00:12:02.201 --> 00:12:05.758
-Yeah, and to me,
it's like I hardly even notice.

00:12:06.094 --> 00:12:07.261
-Dude, I mean, okay...

00:12:07.261 --> 00:12:11.337
So if you live here and I'm sure
the cold water thing gets old.

00:12:11.337 --> 00:12:14.890
If it lasts for a long time,
say, in the summer in your apartment

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:16.552
but I honestly...

00:12:16.552 --> 00:12:18.566
I seriously think
that stuff's good for you

00:12:18.566 --> 00:12:21.715
because it breaks you out of...
It's like Ramadan in Islam, right?

00:12:21.715 --> 00:12:23.527
-Yeah.
-Like you have to fast.

00:12:23.527 --> 00:12:25.584
You have to abstain from sex.

00:12:25.584 --> 00:12:27.739
You have to do all these things
that you normally do.

00:12:27.739 --> 00:12:29.668
You have to abstain
from all these things, right?

00:12:29.668 --> 00:12:34.431
And then when you get those things back
it feels great and you appreciate them.

00:12:34.431 --> 00:12:36.914
And I honestly feel like
the few times the power's gone out...

00:12:36.914 --> 00:12:40.073
Actually it's only happened
like twice in a little over a year.

00:12:40.073 --> 00:12:41.780
My girlfriend and I put the candles on.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:43.197
It was super cool.

00:12:43.197 --> 00:12:47.760
Or the cold shower thing,
I actually don't mind really at all.

00:12:48.178 --> 00:12:49.287
So I think these, like...

00:12:49.287 --> 00:12:52.072
These breaks in the comfort...

00:12:52.072 --> 00:12:54.927
At the end of the day
are actually a good thing.

00:12:54.927 --> 00:12:58.105
I mean if we're talking about these little
things we're talking about right now.

00:12:58.105 --> 00:12:58.876
-Yeah.

00:12:58.876 --> 00:13:01.851
-I remember when
I first moved into your place...

00:13:01.851 --> 00:13:04.692
'cause we lived together, what?
Two months?

00:13:04.692 --> 00:13:06.439
She came barging through the door.

00:13:06.439 --> 00:13:09.022
Started yelling at you for money.

00:13:09.022 --> 00:13:11.000
-In Russian, don't know
a word she's saying

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:13.807
and she's yelling at me in Russian
as if I understand something.

00:13:13.807 --> 00:13:15.541
-I guess that's your fault, right?

00:13:15.541 --> 00:13:18.111
Like, 'cause you're in Ukraine
and you don’t' speak the language.

00:13:18.111 --> 00:13:19.451
That's our fault really, right?

00:13:19.451 --> 00:13:21.834
But at the beginning
you're not gonna get the language

00:13:21.834 --> 00:13:23.820
but what she did was like...

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.318
It's like that American guy in Italy.

00:13:26.318 --> 00:13:27.685
You ever seen that guy?

00:13:27.685 --> 00:13:29.845
With the white socks pulled up to the knee

00:13:29.845 --> 00:13:32.147
and the shorts on
and someone doesn't understand him

00:13:32.147 --> 00:13:35.820
so he starts speaking
louder and slower and I'm like,

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:39.612
"Hey Rick, no matter how loud
and slowly you speak..."

00:13:39.612 --> 00:13:42.139
"If somebody doesn't understand
the language they won't get it."

00:13:42.139 --> 00:13:43.536
and that's how she was to you.

00:13:43.536 --> 00:13:45.050
She was just like...

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:46.672
-And not slow.
-Not slow.

00:13:46.672 --> 00:13:49.261
-Emotional outburst.
-Emotional outburst. [both chuckling]

00:13:49.261 --> 00:13:52.555
And she wanted money, and you had
a stack of hundreds in your hands...

00:13:52.555 --> 00:13:53.872
Like, "Here's the money."

00:13:53.872 --> 00:13:57.592
but she just wanted to rip you apart
for the reason of ripping you apart.

00:13:57.592 --> 00:14:00.851
-They brought a translator that time and...

00:14:01.764 --> 00:14:04.122
I asked the translator, like,

00:14:04.122 --> 00:14:05.675
"Why is she yelling?"

00:14:05.675 --> 00:14:06.729
and he's like,

00:14:06.729 --> 00:14:08.413
"Oh, she thought you weren't gonna pay."

00:14:08.413 --> 00:14:12.434
and I'm like, "Well, clearly the money
is here right now. Why is she still angry?

00:14:12.434 --> 00:14:15.184
He's like, "Oh, she just had
a long flight from Chicago."

00:14:15.184 --> 00:14:16.184
It's like...

00:14:17.464 --> 00:14:18.599
Like I...

00:14:18.599 --> 00:14:21.098
-Right, that's an excuse.
-She's acting like a spoiled...

00:14:21.098 --> 00:14:22.725
...five-year-old child.

00:14:22.725 --> 00:14:26.026
Cranky... You can't, like, yell at people
because you just were on a plane.

00:14:26.026 --> 00:14:27.442
-Especially when they have what you want.

00:14:27.442 --> 00:14:29.542
So you feel like you've been
taken advantage of here

00:14:29.542 --> 00:14:31.666
like just by her
or, like, by a lot of people?

00:14:31.666 --> 00:14:32.775
-Mostly just her.

00:14:32.775 --> 00:14:34.972
I think the land lords here
are a little bit sketchier

00:14:34.972 --> 00:14:36.467
than I'm used to in America.

00:14:36.467 --> 00:14:40.972
In America there's a lot of rights for
the people staying in the apartments.

00:14:40.972 --> 00:14:44.172
-Especially in San Francisco.
-A little bit more clean and professional.

00:14:44.172 --> 00:14:48.288
But here I feel like at least
the property owners I've dealt with

00:14:48.288 --> 00:14:50.371
tend to try to be really cheap

00:14:50.371 --> 00:14:52.333
and cut corners whenever
and however they can.

00:14:52.593 --> 00:14:54.589
-What else, anything else?

00:14:54.589 --> 00:14:56.952
-The interiors of
some of the buildings here...

00:14:56.952 --> 00:15:01.483
-Uh-huh.
-look like the interiors of...

00:15:01.483 --> 00:15:05.784
...a gothic, run-down, punk party.

00:15:05.784 --> 00:15:07.487
-Like the entryways, right?

00:15:07.487 --> 00:15:09.624
-Yeah,
and the elevators and stairways.

00:15:09.624 --> 00:15:12.498
The buildings are beautiful
from the exterior

00:15:12.498 --> 00:15:13.920
and the insides can be beautiful

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:16.484
but the common areas
look like abandoned buildings.

00:15:16.484 --> 00:15:18.424
-Yeah, yeah, yeah,
like going into your place...

00:15:18.424 --> 00:15:20.966
So it's, like, in a nice part of the city.

00:15:20.966 --> 00:15:23.027
It looks beautiful on the outside

00:15:23.027 --> 00:15:26.066
and when I go in your place,
like if I was in Latin America

00:15:26.066 --> 00:15:28.230
I would be fearful of my life.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:31.510
'Cause you see the graffiti,
all the cigarette butts, the trash.

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:33.589
You're like,
"This is not a safe place to be."

00:15:33.589 --> 00:15:37.163
But it's sort of normal here
for a lot of apartment blocks.

00:15:37.163 --> 00:15:38.868
-The last place
I lived as well was like that.

00:15:38.868 --> 00:15:40.875
-And the last place
but then you get on the elevator.

00:15:40.875 --> 00:15:44.636
Which I don't think there are
any bigger threats in this country

00:15:44.636 --> 00:15:47.673
other than these Soviet era elevators.

00:15:48.002 --> 00:15:51.322
Seriously, I think if I die here
that's how it's gonna happen.

00:15:51.322 --> 00:15:52.870
On one of those elevators.

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:57.768
I've had a few experiences
where I definitely felt, um...

00:15:57.768 --> 00:16:01.498
More scared than any roller coaster
or fun thrill-seeking ride.

00:16:01.498 --> 00:16:04.140
Just, it's up there
with skydiving, to be honest.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:07.525
When the elevator comes down,
and, like, jolts

00:16:07.525 --> 00:16:12.166
and it doesn't really go on the level
of the floor you're getting off on...

00:16:12.823 --> 00:16:16.288
I don't know, that to me, is...
Is one of my biggest fears.

00:16:16.288 --> 00:16:17.288
So...

00:16:17.993 --> 00:16:19.569
Okay, what about this?

00:16:19.569 --> 00:16:22.466
It's like a metaphor
for this country, I find.

00:16:22.466 --> 00:16:27.312
Like, it's not always on the surface
but once you get inside

00:16:27.312 --> 00:16:30.431
it opens up and shows
its true self or its better self.

00:16:30.431 --> 00:16:32.038
-And those entryways...
-Yeah, the contrast.

00:16:32.038 --> 00:16:35.532
Those entryways are really
grungy and dirty

00:16:35.532 --> 00:16:37.452
but then you go into,
perhaps, an apartment

00:16:37.452 --> 00:16:40.911
after like three locks are unlocked
through a bomb-proof door...

00:16:40.911 --> 00:16:42.550
-Or two doors.
-Or two doors.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:45.450
-And then it's a different world inside.
-Yeah

00:16:46.494 --> 00:16:48.082
And this kind of reminds me of that.

00:16:48.082 --> 00:16:50.781
Where yeah, they might not have
as good infrastructure

00:16:50.781 --> 00:16:53.451
but people there get by and they’re happy

00:16:53.451 --> 00:16:56.713
and in America we have infrastructure
but then people just take it for granted

00:16:56.713 --> 00:16:58.298
and it's like there's
no point in even having it

00:16:58.298 --> 00:16:59.656
'cause it's not making your life better

00:16:59.656 --> 00:17:02.520
'cause it doesn't affect
your happiness once you're used to it.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:06.014
-Hmm, so do you think
the whole goal is your happiness?

00:17:06.014 --> 00:17:07.826
That should be your goal in life?

00:17:07.826 --> 00:17:12.078
-Somewhat, I think that the happiness
is always relative where

00:17:12.078 --> 00:17:13.422
you get used to things.

00:17:13.422 --> 00:17:14.986
You're big on this.

00:17:14.986 --> 00:17:16.680
About going to Osypenko.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:21.599
Like, you know by going
somewhere like that it's gonna be hard

00:17:21.599 --> 00:17:24.018
but you're gonna appreciate
other things more because of it.

00:17:24.018 --> 00:17:28.873
-Due that experience will go down,
I think when I'm old and I'm dying...

00:17:28.873 --> 00:17:32.853
Like that will flash through my head as
one of the coolest experiences of my life.

00:17:32.853 --> 00:17:38.416
I'm serious, like that was a very
impressionable, meaningful experience.

00:17:38.416 --> 00:17:40.009
And I was just back there,
I visited the family.

00:17:40.009 --> 00:17:43.017
It's like a second family
I now have in Ukraine.

00:17:43.297 --> 00:17:44.297
Which is amazing.

00:17:44.297 --> 00:17:47.433
I feel way more connected to here
because of that.

00:17:47.614 --> 00:17:51.260
-And from the traditional,
like, human point of view

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:52.730
you should have been miserable.

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:54.886
That should have been
one of the worst experiences of your life

00:17:54.886 --> 00:17:57.786
because you went down in lifestyle.
-Yeah...

00:17:58.233 --> 00:17:59.376
It ranged more.

00:17:59.376 --> 00:18:02.096
I mean I had higher highs
and some lower lows.

00:18:02.096 --> 00:18:03.552
I think that's Ukraine in general.

00:18:03.552 --> 00:18:05.584
My emotions range more in this country.

00:18:05.584 --> 00:18:07.950
Like, I feel more peaks...
[both chuckle]

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.314
...and try to look at everything
as an optimist

00:18:10.314 --> 00:18:12.588
but, like, definitely
harder valleys sometimes.

00:18:12.588 --> 00:18:16.328
You look out the window
and the sky's fusing to the ground

00:18:16.328 --> 00:18:18.534
and it looks bleak...

00:18:19.895 --> 00:18:20.895
but...

00:18:21.679 --> 00:18:22.784
it makes...

00:18:22.784 --> 00:18:25.808
It makes it all more interesting I think.

00:18:25.808 --> 00:18:26.863
Like you think too, right?

00:18:26.863 --> 00:18:29.167
And I think it's also different,
like we have...

00:18:29.167 --> 00:18:30.553
Whenever you're doing something different.

00:18:30.553 --> 00:18:33.825
So if I was from Ukraine,
if I was young from Ukraine

00:18:33.825 --> 00:18:35.346
I'd probably want to go to the states.

00:18:35.346 --> 00:18:37.650
At least for a little bit to check it out

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:39.616
or Western Europe just to see
'cause I'm curious.

00:18:39.616 --> 00:18:41.254
I'm a curious person.

00:18:41.254 --> 00:18:42.254
But, uh...

00:18:42.534 --> 00:18:44.016
Yeah, I think when you level things up

00:18:44.016 --> 00:18:46.206
you balance things out
like you were talking in the beginning

00:18:46.206 --> 00:18:48.673
like, "Okay, this, this, that, that."

00:18:49.535 --> 00:18:53.354
For me it works well here,
and home, I have a good life.

00:18:53.354 --> 00:18:55.808
In the US, I mean,
I still have a business there.

00:18:55.808 --> 00:18:58.517
I have family, friends.

00:18:58.517 --> 00:19:00.520
There's tons of opportunity.

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:02.368
It's a beautiful country

00:19:02.368 --> 00:19:03.505
but I know it.

00:19:03.505 --> 00:19:04.919
I know how it works.

00:19:04.919 --> 00:19:07.927
There's no...
My curiosity is not piqued at all.

00:19:08.279 --> 00:19:09.612
I know it's around every...

00:19:09.612 --> 00:19:11.453
You know, like,
you know what's around every turn

00:19:11.453 --> 00:19:13.122
You know how it's gonna operate.

00:19:13.122 --> 00:19:14.813
You know the language.

00:19:14.813 --> 00:19:16.551
You know the behavioralisms.

00:19:16.551 --> 00:19:18.414
Here, is like a...

00:19:18.508 --> 00:19:20.501
A little bit of an adventure in the jungle.

00:19:20.501 --> 00:19:21.988
-You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:19:21.988 --> 00:19:24.134
-And, like, you're swingin'
from some ropes sometimes

00:19:24.134 --> 00:19:25.754
and you don't know
exactly where you're gonna land.

00:19:25.754 --> 00:19:27.288
-You literally don't know
what's gonna be around the corner.

00:19:27.288 --> 00:19:30.076
Whereas in America you can
kind of predict pretty comfortably

00:19:30.076 --> 00:19:32.155
what's gonna be around the corner.

00:19:32.155 --> 00:19:34.052
-Because there's more time...

00:19:34.052 --> 00:19:37.806
I mean it's not to say people
aren't working long schedules

00:19:37.806 --> 00:19:40.652
or busy, or super stressed
but it's different.

00:19:40.652 --> 00:19:44.785
I can't really put a thumb on it
but it's different in a sense that

00:19:44.785 --> 00:19:47.181
in general, people have
more introspection time.

00:19:47.181 --> 00:19:51.779
The look more into deeper things,
into deeper thoughts.

00:19:51.779 --> 00:19:53.218
I'm speaking in generalities.

00:19:53.218 --> 00:19:55.550
Where in the US
it feels more like a hamster wheel.

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:58.396
You're constantly
running, running, running forward.

00:19:58.396 --> 00:19:59.712
Which is great for some things.

00:19:59.712 --> 00:20:01.043
It's great for innovation.

00:20:01.043 --> 00:20:02.723
It's great for startups.

00:20:03.234 --> 00:20:06.093
It's great for
progressing business, right?

00:20:06.093 --> 00:20:08.219
But does it lead to
overall happiness in life?

00:20:08.219 --> 00:20:09.784
That's a different question, I don't know.

00:20:09.784 --> 00:20:13.045
-Yeah, and I think you're
touching on an important point about

00:20:13.045 --> 00:20:18.197
in America, having that specialization,
division of labor, Economics 101.

00:20:18.499 --> 00:20:23.049
But here, people know literature,
philosophy, history.

00:20:23.049 --> 00:20:25.480
So much stuff way better
than the average American

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:29.540
and for somebody, myself,
like, I really enjoy liberal arts.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:32.072
When I was in university
I went to a pure business school

00:20:32.072 --> 00:20:33.854
and half the school was just like,

00:20:33.854 --> 00:20:36.241
"What's the point of learning this
if it doesn't make money?".

00:20:36.241 --> 00:20:38.774
-People here wouldn't say that.
-Right.

00:20:39.389 --> 00:20:42.030
Poets are put up on a pedestal here.

00:20:42.030 --> 00:20:45.275
That's, like, I never even knew
that people still appreciated poets.

00:20:45.275 --> 00:20:47.748
I thought that was something
from like hundreds of years ago.

00:20:47.748 --> 00:20:48.862
[both laughing]

00:20:48.862 --> 00:20:53.141
Literally, there's more statues of poets
than there are of presidents in the city.

00:20:53.141 --> 00:20:56.806
-I remember when
I visited you the first time.

00:20:56.806 --> 00:20:59.564
I was talking to some...
I think he was like 23 years old.

00:20:59.564 --> 00:21:02.349
We were talking about
the FARC in Columbia, right?

00:21:02.349 --> 00:21:04.029
The Revolutionary Guard.

00:21:04.029 --> 00:21:07.758
We're talking about geopolitics,
we're talking about art

00:21:07.758 --> 00:21:11.214
we were talking about surrealism,
we were talking about literature

00:21:11.214 --> 00:21:14.755
we're talking about life philosophy and...

00:21:16.418 --> 00:21:20.266
In general, people have
a wider reach of knowledge.

00:21:20.266 --> 00:21:21.338
Maybe that's just Kyiv.

00:21:21.338 --> 00:21:24.638
I don't know
but in the capital it feels that way.

00:21:24.638 --> 00:21:28.222
Where in America if you want to survive
you have to specialize like crazy.

00:21:28.222 --> 00:21:32.770
Like, I guarantee you in the US
there is a guy that knows spoons

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:33.786
better than anyone.

00:21:33.786 --> 00:21:35.919
He's devoted his life to spoons.

00:21:36.179 --> 00:21:39.707
He knows every.... No one's gonna
touch him in that space, right?

00:21:39.707 --> 00:21:43.251
But he might not know
where Ukraine is on a map.

00:21:43.797 --> 00:21:46.463
Or other basic knowledge, right?

00:21:46.463 --> 00:21:48.270
-My dad is a flashlight guy.

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:52.715
He literally has probably a couple dozen
patents just related to flashlights.

00:21:52.715 --> 00:21:53.539
-Okay.

00:21:53.539 --> 00:21:55.196
So how's someone gonna compete with that?

00:21:55.196 --> 00:21:57.849
-[John chuckles]
In the US, people are comfortable.

00:21:57.849 --> 00:22:02.514
Really comfortable
and comfortable people don't do that much

00:22:02.514 --> 00:22:03.498
and...

00:22:03.498 --> 00:22:05.698
-Yeah, there are a lot of people
not comfortable though, man.

00:22:05.698 --> 00:22:08.662
There are a lot of poor people in the US.

00:22:08.662 --> 00:22:10.547
There's a lot of drug issues.

00:22:10.547 --> 00:22:12.308
-That's true.
-There are a lot of people on the street.

00:22:12.308 --> 00:22:16.716
There are way more homeless people
on the streets of San Francisco than Kyiv.

00:22:16.716 --> 00:22:19.797
I tell my Ukrainian friends that
and they have a hard time believing that

00:22:19.797 --> 00:22:22.663
but yeah, in the tech center of...

00:22:23.472 --> 00:22:24.658
You know...

00:22:24.658 --> 00:22:28.695
Google, Facebook, Yelp,
all these companies, YouTube

00:22:28.695 --> 00:22:29.917
It's like...

00:22:29.917 --> 00:22:34.010
Here is way more sound as far as

