00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:37.434
to start your tech company.

00:33:37.434 --> 00:33:41.146
When that's pretty much
where it's all happening right now.

00:33:41.675 --> 00:33:45.509
-Yeah, it's hard at first
because a little bit of language barrier

00:33:45.509 --> 00:33:47.217
and also just not knowing a lot of people.

00:33:47.217 --> 00:33:49.207
Not knowing "Where do I post jobs?".

00:33:49.207 --> 00:33:52.073
-But why? Why then?
Why would you do it here?

00:33:52.793 --> 00:33:55.326
-I would open a company here because...

00:33:55.785 --> 00:33:57.156
-Well, you have.

00:33:57.156 --> 00:33:58.737
You've got your team going here.

00:33:58.737 --> 00:33:59.939
-The people...

00:33:59.939 --> 00:34:08.448
The developers in Ukraine are smart,
they're creative, but they work at prices

00:34:08.448 --> 00:34:13.032
that are way more affordable
than San Francisco or California.

00:34:13.032 --> 00:34:13.670
-Right.

00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:17.787
-For equal price, it's just simple
supply, demand business 101.

00:34:18.285 --> 00:34:20.589
And especially as a startup
those things matter extra

00:34:20.589 --> 00:34:25.407
because unlike Coca-Cola who has,
basically, infinite money to throw around

00:34:25.407 --> 00:34:28.460
or Apple, who's sitting on
tens of billions of dollars.

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:29.236
-Right.

00:34:29.236 --> 00:34:33.646
-A startup, by it's nature,
it's biggest weakness

00:34:33.646 --> 00:34:36.843
is that it just doesn't have
all the cash and resources.

00:34:36.843 --> 00:34:41.784
I've worked with people in China,
and in India, and a lot of other countries

00:34:41.784 --> 00:34:43.768
and a lot of the times if there's something

00:34:43.768 --> 00:34:45.763
not in the specifications,
they just won't do it.

00:34:45.763 --> 00:34:48.561
Like, if it's obvious there should be
a back button they just won't put it in

00:34:48.561 --> 00:34:51.296
but here people will push back,
they will add things that are common sense.

00:34:51.296 --> 00:34:53.263
-They're more intuitive?
-Yes.

00:34:53.263 --> 00:34:54.719
And I appreciate that a lot.

00:34:54.719 --> 00:34:56.708
Where I'm not much of a micro manager.

00:34:56.708 --> 00:35:00.104
I'm a big picture thinker
and I can't do all the details

00:35:00.104 --> 00:35:01.978
and think of
everything else in the startup.

00:35:01.978 --> 00:35:04.468
So having people here that are
more like co-founders

00:35:04.468 --> 00:35:07.195
than just cheap labor is really nice.

00:35:07.195 --> 00:35:09.208
-Right, and you're team is all...

00:35:09.208 --> 00:35:13.216
I mean it helps for you that you're in an
industry that a lot of young people like.

00:35:13.216 --> 00:35:14.457
Which is gaming.

00:35:14.457 --> 00:35:19.219
So your team seems to me,
from whom I've talked to

00:35:19.219 --> 00:35:21.743
excluding Igor,
who said he quits by the way.

00:35:21.743 --> 00:35:23.263
You weren't around.

00:35:23.287 --> 00:35:24.776
[silence]

00:35:26.949 --> 00:35:28.175
But...

00:35:28.175 --> 00:35:30.411
They seem pretty fired up
and passionate about the project

00:35:30.411 --> 00:35:35.544
'cause they love gaming
and you're providing that outlet.

00:35:35.685 --> 00:35:38.568
It's really fortunate
to be doing that space.

00:35:38.568 --> 00:35:39.401
-Yeah.

00:35:39.401 --> 00:35:43.134
-Overall, what I'm working on is
a much bigger vision than just gaming.

00:35:43.134 --> 00:35:44.792
The game is a good entry point

00:35:44.792 --> 00:35:48.407
and there's a lot of things in life
that there's a lot of correlations.

00:35:48.407 --> 00:35:50.392
Like there's certain kinds
of games that you'll like.

00:35:50.392 --> 00:35:52.963
Certain kinds of people, you'll have
different kinds of relationships with.

00:35:52.963 --> 00:35:55.056
There's a lot of algorithms
based on these buckets.

00:35:55.056 --> 00:35:57.385
It's not a hard science
but it's useful to have mental models.

00:35:57.385 --> 00:36:00.863
-My girlfriend's personality type matches
my personality type well, do you think?

00:36:00.863 --> 00:36:02.959
-For example, you're an extrovert
and she's an introvert.

00:36:02.959 --> 00:36:04.392
-That's pretty common.
-Right.

00:36:04.392 --> 00:36:07.760
-And it seems like it's counter
to a lot of the conventional wisdom.

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:09.975
Like, "Be with somebody the same as you
or similar to you."

00:36:09.975 --> 00:36:12.092
But a nice thing is that

00:36:12.092 --> 00:36:15.061
you like being more talkative
and if there's somebody more quiet

00:36:15.061 --> 00:36:18.354
who enjoys listening a bit more
then that creates a good synergy.

00:36:18.354 --> 00:36:21.211
-There's a good... Yeah, there's no
competition between us in anything.

00:36:21.211 --> 00:36:24.043
Like, I fill in where she's not there.

00:36:24.043 --> 00:36:25.649
She fills in where I'm not.
You know what I mean?

00:36:25.649 --> 00:36:26.501
-Yeah.

00:36:26.501 --> 00:36:27.703
And so that stuff's really useful

00:36:27.703 --> 00:36:30.317
considering how important
people, and communication,

00:36:30.317 --> 00:36:31.917
and meeting people are in our lives.

00:36:31.917 --> 00:36:33.954
It's something that gets
very disproportionately

00:36:33.954 --> 00:36:35.524
small amounts of attention.

00:36:35.524 --> 00:36:38.019
And so a lot of what GameTree is about is

00:36:38.019 --> 00:36:41.808
using this knowledge to educate us
about ourselves, our predispositions.

00:36:41.808 --> 00:36:43.828
Knowing the lens,
the microscopes, the telescopes

00:36:43.828 --> 00:36:45.365
that we're seeing the world through

00:36:45.365 --> 00:36:47.632
and having clear
understanding of ourselves.

00:36:47.632 --> 00:36:49.198
How we're biased to view other people.

00:36:49.198 --> 00:36:50.834
How other people are viewed.

00:36:50.834 --> 00:36:52.470
How to appreciate those kinds of people.

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:53.832
There's just so many levels.

00:36:53.832 --> 00:36:55.816
-So through your app...

00:36:55.816 --> 00:36:57.690
Someone's interested in a game and they can

00:36:57.690 --> 00:37:00.644
meet someone that
has their same personality type

00:37:00.644 --> 00:37:02.022
or a different personality type.

00:37:02.022 --> 00:37:04.116
At least they know
and they can connect with that person.

00:37:04.116 --> 00:37:07.080
-So they can find
specific kinds of people saying, like,

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:09.723
"Oh, I have this friend group and I want
somebody who's kind of like this."

00:37:09.723 --> 00:37:12.510
They could actually filter
and find these exact kinds of people

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:15.520
in ways that you could never
otherwise meet so easily.

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:18.556
Knowing this personality psychology
with gaming is especially interesting

00:37:18.556 --> 00:37:20.627
because you can see
what's fun for somebody.

00:37:20.627 --> 00:37:22.391
That correlates highly
with their psychology.

00:37:22.391 --> 00:37:24.513
Not everybody has
the same definition of fun

00:37:24.513 --> 00:37:29.174
but as children, we play,
we grow, and develop through games.

00:37:29.174 --> 00:37:29.740
-Sure.

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:31.037
-And so in a way, it's a great way

00:37:31.037 --> 00:37:33.861
to find friends, build relationships,
and continue growing

00:37:33.861 --> 00:37:37.104
And there's a strange
social stigma right now around that

00:37:37.104 --> 00:37:39.462
that makes that seem less appropriate

00:37:39.462 --> 00:37:42.005
but at the same time,
play is what makes people happy

00:37:42.005 --> 00:37:44.627
and creates a lot of
the most authentic relationships.

00:37:44.944 --> 00:37:47.787
-What do you think of
the art situation here?

00:37:47.787 --> 00:37:51.057
-I find that where
there's a lot of emotions

00:37:51.057 --> 00:37:53.323
you find a lot of the most interesting art.

00:37:53.347 --> 00:37:54.930
A lot of the...

00:37:55.618 --> 00:37:59.266
The source material that creates poetry,
that creates analogies

00:37:59.266 --> 00:38:00.953
comes from our feelings

00:38:00.953 --> 00:38:05.104
and because Ukraine's in a place
where there's such stark contrasts

00:38:05.104 --> 00:38:07.292
and there's revolutions
and there's a war going on

00:38:07.292 --> 00:38:09.975
that this is a place
that has a lot of emotions.

00:38:10.508 --> 00:38:13.131
And so I think that that's the reason why

00:38:13.131 --> 00:38:17.128
there's so many
beautiful installations and graffiti

00:38:17.128 --> 00:38:20.185
and people trying to express
all these things that they're feeling.

00:38:20.185 --> 00:38:22.549
-I find...
Okay, another culture that does this...

00:38:22.549 --> 00:38:24.884
I noticed in Egypt after their revolution

00:38:24.884 --> 00:38:28.258
the contemporary art museum there
was mind-blowing.

00:38:28.258 --> 00:38:32.414
It was all from the youth
all art from the youth, and similar to...

00:38:32.414 --> 00:38:33.758
The art's different but it's similar

00:38:33.758 --> 00:38:36.949
in the sense that you have
the youth doing a ton of...

00:38:36.949 --> 00:38:38.563
At least these art installations...

00:38:38.563 --> 00:38:41.248
There aren't a lot of museums here,
there are a few

00:38:41.248 --> 00:38:43.875
but it's definitely...
It's coming out them.

00:38:43.875 --> 00:38:45.437
Which is cool to see on the streets.

00:38:45.437 --> 00:38:50.455
I feel like Southern California
is famous for art, like San Diego

00:38:50.455 --> 00:38:54.166
but the art there is a lot of galleries
and putting your paintings up

00:38:54.166 --> 00:38:56.816
and it's like, "I want to be an artist
so I can paint things."

00:38:56.816 --> 00:38:59.126
but I feel like the artists here...

00:38:59.804 --> 00:39:04.920
It's like, "I have to express something
and I'm gonna do it however I can."

00:39:04.920 --> 00:39:06.926
and that creates a really authentic, raw

00:39:06.926 --> 00:39:09.935
very easy to appreciate,
emotionally rich kind of art.

00:39:09.935 --> 00:39:11.306
That is fascinating.

00:39:11.306 --> 00:39:12.721
That I did not expect here.

00:39:12.721 --> 00:39:15.995
-Well I think you get to a point
in the human experience

00:39:15.995 --> 00:39:21.117
where you have to get
what's inside of you out or it kills you.

00:39:21.117 --> 00:39:25.680
So for example, nothing I do right now
brings me monetary reward.

00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:30.859
Like this or, like, writing,
but I have to get it out of me

00:39:30.859 --> 00:39:34.306
or there's this pent up
frustration inside me.

00:39:34.306 --> 00:39:36.086
-We're taught to be consumers.

00:39:36.086 --> 00:39:37.488
We're a consumer society

00:39:37.488 --> 00:39:40.608
but every person is just as much
a creator as a consumer

00:39:40.608 --> 00:39:46.360
and it sucks when the most fun part of life
is sort of swept under the rug.

00:39:46.360 --> 00:39:50.341
Where suddenly instead of being somebody
who's making shoes for your friends

00:39:50.341 --> 00:39:53.921
you're a person is Southeast Asia
working in a shoe factory

00:39:53.921 --> 00:39:56.728
with harsh conditions living miserably.

00:39:56.728 --> 00:40:00.224
-But don't you...
There's also a level too, where...

00:40:00.224 --> 00:40:03.868
If you consume too much and don't
create enough, there's a void, right?

00:40:03.868 --> 00:40:04.790
-Yeah.

00:40:04.790 --> 00:40:08.486
-I think for me, the more I create
the better I feel actually.

00:40:08.486 --> 00:40:10.544
-It's like you
spend energy to get energy.

00:40:10.544 --> 00:40:11.821
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:40:11.821 --> 00:40:12.822
All right, Juke.

00:40:12.822 --> 00:40:16.469
-One thing I'm really
excited about with GameTree

00:40:16.469 --> 00:40:18.906
is that by giving
personalized recommendations

00:40:18.906 --> 00:40:21.878
based on people's tastes
and on their psychologies

00:40:21.878 --> 00:40:25.952
you can discover the really niche part
that really speaks to you the most.

00:40:25.952 --> 00:40:26.605
-Right.

00:40:26.605 --> 00:40:28.006
-That you might never know about

00:40:28.006 --> 00:40:30.682
through all the mainstream
large-scale advertising

00:40:30.682 --> 00:40:32.757
and at the same time
it's a big opportunity.

00:40:32.757 --> 00:40:35.829
Possibly an unprecedented opportunity
for small artists

00:40:35.829 --> 00:40:38.332
to actually make a living
or to get some traction

00:40:38.332 --> 00:40:39.590
doing the things that they're doing.

00:40:39.590 --> 00:40:42.596
Whether you're making films,
or fashion, or video games

00:40:42.596 --> 00:40:47.256
you can't afford to do the AAA advertising
because it's a really niche product..

00:40:47.256 --> 00:40:49.293
The production budget isn't there

00:40:49.293 --> 00:40:52.707
but if through GameTree
and eventually expanding this

00:40:52.707 --> 00:40:54.363
to be recommendations for all things.

00:40:54.363 --> 00:40:55.825
It's actually a massive opportunity

00:40:55.825 --> 00:40:57.849
for artists to make a living
doing the things that they love

00:40:57.849 --> 00:41:00.949
and for people to consume
higher levels of content.

00:41:00.949 --> 00:41:02.617
Where they can actually
engage with the artists

00:41:02.617 --> 00:41:06.012
and have more real
authentic human connections.

00:41:06.955 --> 00:41:08.315
All right, broski.

00:41:10.034 --> 00:41:11.609
PETER: Okay, Ukraine is...

00:41:11.609 --> 00:41:15.842
Let's face it,
it's known in the world for corruption.

00:41:15.842 --> 00:41:17.423
From the top levels.

00:41:17.423 --> 00:41:20.438
I think it permeates
all the way through society here.

00:41:20.438 --> 00:41:22.808
As a startup founder...

00:41:23.761 --> 00:41:26.444
As a person new to this country

00:41:26.444 --> 00:41:30.028
have you dealt with any issues
as far as corruption goes?

00:41:30.325 --> 00:41:32.924
-Fortunately, it's been
a complete non-issue here.

00:41:32.949 --> 00:41:33.949
-Yeah.

00:41:34.272 --> 00:41:37.195
-Very luckily, but I do know it exists

00:41:37.195 --> 00:41:39.877
and I've heard instances,
and I know Ukrainians who it affects

00:41:39.877 --> 00:41:41.884
and it seems to really suck a lot.

00:41:41.884 --> 00:41:47.088
For example I know somebody
whose family home...

00:41:47.088 --> 00:41:50.938
is falling apart.
-Yeah.

00:41:50.938 --> 00:41:53.642
-There's cracks in the wall,
refrigerator stopped working

00:41:53.642 --> 00:41:58.214
because they're building a giant illegal
structure that's not zoned for the area

00:41:58.214 --> 00:42:01.916
and her family complained
to every possible agency.

00:42:01.916 --> 00:42:04.995
The police, like,
all the government institutions

00:42:04.995 --> 00:42:07.044
and nobody was doing anything

00:42:07.044 --> 00:42:12.202
and eventually
the police responded and said,

00:42:12.202 --> 00:42:14.907
"Just drop it,
there's powerful people building this..."

00:42:14.907 --> 00:42:17.113
"...and it's good if you just
don't do anything about it."

00:42:17.113 --> 00:42:21.209
and some activist, like a local activist
got involved and was helping...

00:42:21.209 --> 00:42:21.925
-Uh-huh.

00:42:21.925 --> 00:42:26.495
-...and her tires got slashed and her cat
was found dead in her back yard, murdered.

00:42:26.495 --> 00:42:27.873
And...

00:42:27.873 --> 00:42:32.342
That's so f*cked up that you have
very blatant transgressions of the law.

00:42:32.342 --> 00:42:34.576
Where everybody is just
turning the other cheek.

00:42:34.576 --> 00:42:36.045
That doesn't happen in America.

00:42:36.045 --> 00:42:37.335
For all of our vices...

00:42:37.335 --> 00:42:39.395
We have, like, legal corruption

00:42:39.395 --> 00:42:43.921
but just to have something
so blatantly illegal going on

00:42:43.921 --> 00:42:46.848
is really infuriating.

00:42:46.848 --> 00:42:49.466
That people are
getting away with stuff like that.

00:42:49.466 --> 00:42:51.445
-But it doesn't affect you currently?

00:42:51.445 --> 00:42:55.702
-Correct, yeah, like starting the company
here I've had no issues with anything

00:42:55.702 --> 00:42:56.702
-Okay.

00:42:56.971 --> 00:42:59.982
-If you buy a water or something
at the store, people are ripping...

00:42:59.982 --> 00:43:05.271
-Oh, they don't, even the babushki
on the street that are giving us flowers.

00:43:05.271 --> 00:43:07.790
They could charge us
at least five times more for the flowers

00:43:07.790 --> 00:43:09.870
or at least try,
thinking that we're complete tourists

00:43:09.870 --> 00:43:12.302
but people here have a lot of integrity

00:43:12.302 --> 00:43:16.021
and especially seeing that contrast
having gone to Sri Lanka

00:43:16.021 --> 00:43:17.983
where people have more wealth.

00:43:17.983 --> 00:43:20.586
That people here
tend to have a lot more civility.

00:43:20.785 --> 00:43:22.785
-Yeah, it's weird how that works
in different cultures.

00:43:22.785 --> 00:43:25.119
So if you go to China or Vietnam...

00:43:25.796 --> 00:43:29.161
It's very blatant, they're gonna get
whatever they can out of you

00:43:29.161 --> 00:43:31.244
and that's part of the culture,
it's baked into the culture.

00:43:31.244 --> 00:43:34.892
Even Vietnam had a law,
like, foreigners get charged more.

00:43:35.272 --> 00:43:37.882
That was eradicated
but still in the mentality

00:43:37.882 --> 00:43:40.329
Here, I haven't dealt with it at all.

00:43:40.329 --> 00:43:42.804
Not that I know of, and I'm pretty on it.

00:43:42.804 --> 00:43:46.092
Know the prices pretty well, but I haven't
dealt with anyone trying to rip me off.

00:43:46.092 --> 00:43:50.246
Which is super cool when
you're in a country and you don't, like...

00:43:50.246 --> 00:43:52.955
I think that's the biggest resistance
when you're an outsider

00:43:52.955 --> 00:43:55.339
'cause there is some assimilation process

00:43:55.339 --> 00:43:57.436
and we'll never fully be assimilated.

00:43:57.436 --> 00:43:59.921
We'll never be Ukrainian
by any stretch, right?

00:43:59.921 --> 00:44:03.752
Even if you and I speak perfect Ukrainian
we'll never be Ukrainian, right?

00:44:03.752 --> 00:44:09.871
And there's an assimilation
or a welcoming by not trying to rip you off

00:44:09.871 --> 00:44:10.996
in every transaction.

00:44:10.996 --> 00:44:15.496
When you get ripped off in every
transaction you make it's just, like...

00:44:15.496 --> 00:44:17.609
It makes you feel like an outsider.

00:44:17.609 --> 00:44:18.705
There's no way around it.

00:44:19.807 --> 00:44:21.649
Here's another
good point of this country.

00:44:21.649 --> 00:44:27.456
They're not gonna deal with any
radical, fundamentalist, Islamic BS.

00:44:27.456 --> 00:44:30.179
This is not on ISIS's map.

00:44:31.005 --> 00:44:33.173
Kyiv, you know what I mean?
-You're not a target here.

00:44:33.173 --> 00:44:36.213
-Nah, like Western Europe, America.

00:44:36.213 --> 00:44:38.813
Where there's these cultural frictions.

00:44:39.321 --> 00:44:40.981
There's none of that here.

00:44:40.981 --> 00:44:42.375
I have to say it's pretty homogenous.

00:44:42.375 --> 00:44:45.575
It's probably, what?
98% white and Ukrainian?

00:44:45.575 --> 00:44:47.765
I would guess something like that.

00:44:47.765 --> 00:44:51.428
There are no racial issues
just because there are no races really.

00:44:51.428 --> 00:44:53.858
-That's actually one of the perks,
I think, to working here.

00:44:53.858 --> 00:44:57.348
It sounds horribly
politically incorrect to say

00:44:57.348 --> 00:45:01.038
but it's actually a lot easier
to operate a company

00:45:01.038 --> 00:45:05.070
where I don't have to be
as concerned about there being, like...

00:45:05.349 --> 00:45:07.936
A black person,
and, like, a Chinese person,

00:45:07.936 --> 00:45:11.644
and just, like, in every shot
or every picture.

00:45:12.816 --> 00:45:15.819
-Yeah, so I think
in this space we live in

00:45:15.819 --> 00:45:18.876
let's just call it a vacuum
'cause in a way it is.

00:45:18.876 --> 00:45:23.731
We don't have to cohere
to home and expectations

00:45:23.731 --> 00:45:25.924
and the cultural norms, right?

00:45:26.437 --> 00:45:28.445
And we're not Ukrainian
and we never will be.

00:45:28.445 --> 00:45:30.336
So you're in this space as a foreigner.

00:45:30.336 --> 00:45:33.147
I think that's why a lot of expats like it.

00:45:33.147 --> 00:45:36.477
Some are escaping...
A lot are escaping something, right?

00:45:36.477 --> 00:45:37.477
For sure.

00:45:37.477 --> 00:45:40.927
Maybe I'm escaping the fact that, um...

00:45:41.925 --> 00:45:43.769
I don't want to be around
the same conditions

00:45:43.769 --> 00:45:45.699
that I've been around for a long time.

00:45:45.699 --> 00:45:47.440
They're just not as interesting, right?

00:45:47.440 --> 00:45:51.596
Like, I want the adventure
and my curiosity piqued

00:45:51.596 --> 00:45:54.064
and that exists here
where it doesn't exist at home anymore

00:45:54.064 --> 00:45:56.084
'cause I understand how the thing works.

00:45:56.084 --> 00:45:58.742
The system, the language,
what's around the next turn.

00:45:58.742 --> 00:46:00.005
You get how it works.

00:46:00.005 --> 00:46:03.667
Here I don't totally get it
so there's mystery in that, intrigue

00:46:03.667 --> 00:46:05.078
and then I'm challenged.

00:46:05.078 --> 00:46:06.078
So...

00:46:07.281 --> 00:46:11.250
Yeah, I just find by being a foreigner
in a country like Ukraine

00:46:11.250 --> 00:46:13.760
you can live a very free existence.

00:46:13.760 --> 00:46:15.384
The marketing machine
doesn't hit us, right?

00:46:15.384 --> 00:46:19.126
'Cause I can't read
these Ukrainian ads, right?

00:46:19.126 --> 00:46:20.575
I don't watch TV.

00:46:20.575 --> 00:46:22.548
So none of these messages...

00:46:22.548 --> 00:46:24.837
Like, America's the worst for this, right?

00:46:24.837 --> 00:46:26.167
It's the worst and the best, right?

00:46:26.167 --> 00:46:30.253
It's the best marketing
and advertising machine there is, right?

00:46:30.253 --> 00:46:35.637
And it's so curated into our society
and the fabric of our every day life

00:46:35.637 --> 00:46:39.049
that you become
sort of immune to it, right?

00:46:39.049 --> 00:46:42.522
But also it's constructing how you,
sort of, operate in a way

00:46:42.522 --> 00:46:45.397
and here it's like all that is removed.

00:46:46.022 --> 00:46:48.586
Like, there's no marketing coming at me.

00:46:48.586 --> 00:46:50.626
I don't know the big brands here.

00:46:50.626 --> 00:46:52.983
-I don't really care.
-It's less distractions.

00:46:52.983 --> 00:46:55.680
-So many less distractions
and also shopping...

00:46:55.680 --> 00:46:58.580
Shopping is
an absolute nightmare here, right?

00:46:58.953 --> 00:46:59.970
Um...

00:46:59.970 --> 00:47:02.000
The big store here, what is it called?

00:47:02.000 --> 00:47:03.743
Metro, or something?

00:47:03.743 --> 00:47:04.744
Metro?

00:47:04.744 --> 00:47:06.096
I don’t know, one of the big box shops.

00:47:06.096 --> 00:47:07.758
It's like the Walmart, right?

00:47:07.758 --> 00:47:09.158
It's so poorly organized.

00:47:09.158 --> 00:47:11.800
There's, like, a fork in this department

00:47:11.800 --> 00:47:15.661
and then half kilometer away
there's another fork, right?

00:47:15.661 --> 00:47:17.516
And it's so frustrating to shop in that.

00:47:17.516 --> 00:47:20.006
I hope to never go again.

00:47:20.217 --> 00:47:21.517
And things are just...

00:47:21.517 --> 00:47:23.056
There's no Amazon culture here.

00:47:23.056 --> 00:47:24.952
There's no purchasing immediately.

00:47:24.952 --> 00:47:28.723
Some impulse purchase online
is showing up at your house that day

00:47:28.723 --> 00:47:30.626
and you can return it if you want, right?

00:47:30.626 --> 00:47:34.628
So, like, I hate shopping
but the whole materialism thing

00:47:34.628 --> 00:47:35.988
is somewhat gone.

00:47:36.440 --> 00:47:37.480
Like removed.

00:47:37.729 --> 00:47:41.630
-Something I do notice
about people here is there are a lot of

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:45.722
really talented artists,
engineers, creative types

00:47:45.722 --> 00:47:47.963
but they're missing the business

00:47:47.963 --> 00:47:52.901
and I don't know if it's from
the Soviet Union not doing that

00:47:52.901 --> 00:47:55.729
or kind of being sided with Russia.

00:47:55.729 --> 00:47:57.610
Not accessing Western markets a lot

00:47:57.610 --> 00:48:00.580
but people here build a lot of great things
that don't make it out of the country.

00:48:00.580 --> 00:48:03.229
In America we have very few
products and companies

00:48:03.229 --> 00:48:04.734
that are coming from Eastern Europe

00:48:04.734 --> 00:48:07.482
proportionate to how much
of the population of the West they make up.

00:48:07.482 --> 00:48:08.247
-Sure.

00:48:08.247 --> 00:48:10.612
-I think the American dream is the idea

00:48:10.612 --> 00:48:13.287
that your children can have
a better life than you have

00:48:13.287 --> 00:48:17.819
and that even if you're born poor,
you can rise up and be somebody

00:48:17.819 --> 00:48:20.510
who's very successful
in one sense or another.

00:48:20.510 --> 00:48:22.519
-And it's still great for that,
I really feel that.

00:48:22.519 --> 00:48:26.076
-It seems like it
but if you look at numbers

00:48:26.076 --> 00:48:28.624
comparing it to Scandinavia,
and Switzerland

00:48:28.624 --> 00:48:30.422
and a lot of other countries

00:48:30.422 --> 00:48:33.584
America is actually sliding down
on that scale quite a lot

00:48:33.584 --> 00:48:37.500
but we have this delusion that
a lot of the Republican Party puts out.

00:48:37.500 --> 00:48:41.317
Where they make people think
that things are individual people's faults

00:48:41.317 --> 00:48:43.653
or that that dream is still alive

00:48:43.653 --> 00:48:46.992
or that not taxing corporations
or doing all these things are good

00:48:46.992 --> 00:48:49.660
but they're just complete,
blatant, unsupported lies.

00:48:49.660 --> 00:48:52.797
-Where do the big innovation makers...

00:48:52.797 --> 00:48:53.997
Where do the...

00:48:54.758 --> 00:48:58.594
Where did the guy that started WhatsApp,
from Ukraine, where did he go?

00:48:58.594 --> 00:49:00.510
Why does everyone go there
if it's so terrible?

00:49:00.510 --> 00:49:03.410
It's the best ecosystem
to bring your idea...

00:49:03.883 --> 00:49:08.139
It's the best cultural ecosystem
to get money for it and to actually...

00:49:08.139 --> 00:49:10.153
It's the best market
to bring it out to the world.

00:49:10.153 --> 00:49:12.420
-America has a lot of risk-takers.

00:49:12.849 --> 00:49:14.562
-It's founded off risk-taking.

00:49:14.562 --> 00:49:18.890
The whole design of the place
is risk-taking, right?

00:49:19.152 --> 00:49:22.460
-People with ADHD,
Attention Deficit Hyperactive Disorder...

00:49:22.460 --> 00:49:24.634
-You have it, right?
-Are more likely to move.

00:49:24.634 --> 00:49:26.228
They're more likely to invent things.

00:49:26.228 --> 00:49:30.055
America, like half the population
practically has ADHD.

00:49:30.055 --> 00:49:30.853
-Yeah, that's...

00:49:30.853 --> 00:49:33.200
-Here, most people
don't even know what that is.

00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:35.091
They've recently proven with brain scans

00:49:35.091 --> 00:49:38.161
that it's actually
a disorder that people have.

00:49:38.161 --> 00:49:39.433
I don't think it's a disorder.

00:49:39.433 --> 00:49:41.404
It's a different way of thinking
that's not as accustomed

00:49:41.404 --> 00:49:43.373
to sitting at a desk
and rote memorizing things.

00:49:43.373 --> 00:49:45.728
It's more about exploring the world,
and being open, and inventive.

00:49:45.728 --> 00:49:46.491
-Sure.

00:49:46.491 --> 00:49:49.145
-But that's considered a mental handicap
in the way our system is

00:49:49.145 --> 00:49:52.494
but that sort of thinking,
when you have more investors

00:49:52.494 --> 00:49:55.450
more innovators, more people
who are supporting entrepreneurs

00:49:55.450 --> 00:49:59.184
and thinking, "Oh, that's cool
that you tried to take a risk."

00:49:59.184 --> 00:50:01.119
Here you have a lot more people
who are like,

00:50:01.119 --> 00:50:04.822
"Oh, don't take risks because if there's
another [unclear], you're dead here.

00:50:04.822 --> 00:50:07.715
'Cause a lot of people here,
they don't have strong business skills

00:50:07.715 --> 00:50:09.487
and they don't have
a lot of strong leadership skills.

00:50:09.487 --> 00:50:14.609
In the past, a lot of people
were persecuted or killed for sticking out

00:50:14.609 --> 00:50:16.907
and I think that happened
so much for so many years

00:50:16.907 --> 00:50:19.172
that maybe it's even
suppressed an entire generation.

00:50:19.172 --> 00:50:21.042
For example, Maidan.

00:50:22.162 --> 00:50:25.173
Instead of ending up with a lot of change
and a lot of leaders coming out

00:50:25.173 --> 00:50:28.525
it was just the same deck of cards
shuffled in a different order.

00:50:28.525 --> 00:50:29.525
And so...

00:50:30.765 --> 00:50:33.080
In a way I feel like...

00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:36.176
More foreigners coming here,
even though it's kind of bad

00:50:36.176 --> 00:50:37.711
in that it's not local.

00:50:37.711 --> 00:50:39.357
It's actually really good for the country

00:50:39.357 --> 00:50:41.971
because they're gonna be
bringing in, not just in money

00:50:41.971 --> 00:50:44.538
but they're gonna be mentoring
and teaching these skills

00:50:44.538 --> 00:50:46.054
and inspiring other people around them.

00:50:46.054 --> 00:50:47.816
-Yeah.
-Who otherwise don't have good examples.

00:50:47.816 --> 00:50:53.108
-But okay, no country,
person, place, has it all, right?

00:50:53.108 --> 00:50:58.448
So the strong skill sets here are,
like, you have strong math and sciences.

00:50:58.448 --> 00:51:00.164
I'm speaking in generalities, right?

00:51:00.164 --> 00:51:01.960
Strong math and sciences...

00:51:01.960 --> 00:51:02.960
Umm...

00:51:03.313 --> 00:51:04.742
Intelligent people.

00:51:04.742 --> 00:51:07.410
I find them more philosophical in general.

00:51:07.410 --> 00:51:11.030
They can go deeper into
analytical thinking about different issues.

00:51:11.030 --> 00:51:13.946
Approaching things
from different angles, right?

00:51:13.946 --> 00:51:17.936
And America is better on
the whole capitalism thing obviously

00:51:17.936 --> 00:51:19.914
'cause it's baked into the DNA.

00:51:19.914 --> 00:51:21.642
That's what it's built on, right?

00:51:21.642 --> 00:51:26.309
So yeah, if you can fuse those two together

00:51:26.309 --> 00:51:28.341
I think you have something very powerful.

00:51:28.341 --> 00:51:31.428
-Young people here,
they're very optimistic.

00:51:31.428 --> 00:51:33.468
A lot of them have very good English.

00:51:33.878 --> 00:51:36.025
Ukraine's...
-We're talking about the center of Kyiv.

00:51:36.025 --> 00:51:37.034
-Yeah.
-Right.

00:51:37.034 --> 00:51:38.693
-Ukraine's the third or fourth biggest

00:51:38.693 --> 00:51:40.842
outsourcing country
in the world for technology.

00:51:40.842 --> 00:51:41.515
-Uh-huh.

00:51:41.515 --> 00:51:43.973
-Despite not being
the third or fourth biggest population

00:51:43.973 --> 00:51:49.236
and that makes people here have a lot more
outward focus compared to Russia.

00:51:49.236 --> 00:51:52.957
Where the average programmer
makes more money, for example.

00:51:52.957 --> 00:51:54.588
They have a bigger internal culture,

00:51:54.588 --> 00:51:56.609
and they have their own
supply and demand internally

00:51:56.609 --> 00:52:00.071
but Ukraine is a lot more global
and that's something that's nice.

00:52:00.071 --> 00:52:01.575
Just having that sort of mindset

00:52:01.575 --> 00:52:03.726
and with that comes,
I think, a lot of optimism.

00:52:03.726 --> 00:52:05.925
-You think it's a more open-minded place?

00:52:05.925 --> 00:52:09.413
-I do. A lot of young people here
hold very similar opinions to me

00:52:09.413 --> 00:52:11.360
or to a lot of San Franciscans.

00:52:11.360 --> 00:52:17.051
A lot more than I would think
from a place where not too long ago

00:52:17.051 --> 00:52:19.597
women were, and still are, slightly,

00:52:19.597 --> 00:52:23.267
but being taught to get married
and have babies as soon as possible.

00:52:23.267 --> 00:52:26.500
Usually you find that sort of thing
in third world countries.

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:28.180
-Well it's here for sure.

00:52:28.464 --> 00:52:31.331
Like more than the heart of the Arab world.

00:52:31.563 --> 00:52:34.246
More than Damascus.

00:52:34.246 --> 00:52:36.355
When I spent time there before the war.

00:52:36.355 --> 00:52:37.594
More than Cairo.

00:52:37.594 --> 00:52:40.187
More than, I'd say, Istanbul.

00:52:40.187 --> 00:52:44.596
Like here, there's a pressure on women
to have children very young.

00:52:44.945 --> 00:52:47.033
I don't know where that comes from exactly.

00:52:47.033 --> 00:52:50.727
-It's not everybody but I think
it does exist a fair amount.

00:52:50.727 --> 00:52:55.254
I think a lot of it's from
a lot of men dying during the Soviet Union.

00:52:55.254 --> 00:52:56.827
World War I and II.

00:52:56.827 --> 00:53:00.964
Where if so many of the men
die from different epidemics...

00:53:00.964 --> 00:53:01.745
-Sure.

00:53:01.745 --> 00:53:05.381
-'Cause men disproportionately
die all the time whatever happens

00:53:05.381 --> 00:53:07.656
and then it creates
a lot more competition for men

00:53:07.656 --> 00:53:10.223
and so a lot of the men here,
I think are spoiled.

00:53:10.223 --> 00:53:11.325
They're taking care of a lot.

00:53:11.325 --> 00:53:13.656
The women work hard
and the guys take a lot for granted

00:53:13.656 --> 00:53:16.689
and I think that's part of why
women want Western men

00:53:16.689 --> 00:53:20.209
is because Western men
will try harder to please them.

00:53:20.415 --> 00:53:23.017
Whereas otherwise the guys here
are (disincentivized)

00:53:23.042 --> 00:53:24.851
because it's a disproportionate
amount of women

00:53:24.851 --> 00:53:30.585
and they've been brought up and bred
to take disproportionate care of the men.

00:53:31.460 --> 00:53:34.874
So actually, here,
the personality psychology,

00:53:34.874 --> 00:53:38.024
a lot of it that I follow the most
is actually already really famous.

00:53:38.024 --> 00:53:39.834
It's considered a science
in Eastern Europe.

00:53:39.834 --> 00:53:42.060
Which is known as socionics here.

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:45.368
An interesting contrast
between it here and in the West

00:53:45.368 --> 00:53:50.411
is that here it's mostly used by women
to know the personality dynamics

00:53:50.411 --> 00:53:54.021
about who is a good mate for them
or what are their best partners.

00:53:54.021 --> 00:53:54.643
-Sure.

00:53:54.643 --> 00:53:56.627
-In the West it's mostly about jobs.

00:53:56.627 --> 00:53:57.840
What are the jobs I could have?

00:53:57.840 --> 00:53:59.871
How does this apply
to work and managing people?

00:53:59.871 --> 00:54:02.244
I think that really shows
a lot about the cultures.

00:54:02.244 --> 00:54:05.466
About relationships and happiness
versus work, in that...

00:54:05.466 --> 00:54:06.508
But it's the same framework.

00:54:06.508 --> 00:54:08.117
It's based on Carl Jung.

00:54:08.117 --> 00:54:11.872
Where essentially it's about figuring out,
"How do people think?"

00:54:11.872 --> 00:54:13.471
"What energizes them?"

00:54:13.471 --> 00:54:15.641
and those, by default
become their strongest tools

00:54:15.641 --> 00:54:18.488
and based on how we think,
that's how we're gonna solve problems,

00:54:18.488 --> 00:54:20.683
how we're gonna perceive the world,
and what's fun to us.

00:54:20.859 --> 00:54:21.570
-Right.

00:54:21.570 --> 00:54:24.105
-But what I wasn't expecting as much
is on the soft side.

00:54:24.105 --> 00:54:27.518
For example, during Maidan
I really looked up to the Ukrainians

00:54:27.518 --> 00:54:29.864
thinking, like, "Wow, I really respect
and admire these guys."

00:54:29.864 --> 00:54:30.864
"They're like heroes."

00:54:30.864 --> 00:54:32.553
I like to imagine that I would be somebody

00:54:32.553 --> 00:54:35.324
who would stand up during that time
but you never know...

00:54:35.324 --> 00:54:38.334
But in America you don't really have
those sorts of opportunities.

00:54:38.334 --> 00:54:40.719
The most that's gonna happen is people
clicking share on Facebook

00:54:40.719 --> 00:54:43.193
and grumbling,
and having circular conversations.

00:54:43.193 --> 00:54:46.540
Agreeing with all their friends
about something in bubbles.

00:54:46.540 --> 00:54:49.087
-Yeah, American was
shaken up in 1865.

00:54:49.087 --> 00:54:51.087
That was the last time really.

00:54:51.497 --> 00:54:55.052
But it has its own stresses and weirdness.

00:54:55.052 --> 00:54:57.624
Which is, for example,
all these school shootings.

00:54:57.624 --> 00:54:59.076
That's mental, man.

00:54:59.076 --> 00:55:01.274
Like, that doesn't exist
in this part of the world.

00:55:01.274 --> 00:55:02.930
I haven't heard of anyone walking in...

00:55:02.930 --> 00:55:08.829
A kid walking in with a semi-automatic
and just spraying bullets on other kids.

00:55:08.829 --> 00:55:10.187
That doesn't exist.

00:55:10.187 --> 00:55:13.495
-I think that capitalism was a great system

00:55:13.495 --> 00:55:15.905
that got us through a time
when there was scarcity

00:55:15.905 --> 00:55:18.438
but it's also a disease at this point.

00:55:20.176 --> 00:55:23.511
Any mental illness
can be considered a disease

00:55:23.511 --> 00:55:27.115
as long as it's not appropriate
for the current environment.

00:55:27.115 --> 00:55:27.790
-Mm-hmm.

00:55:27.790 --> 00:55:30.179
-And so while capitalism
was a good system for our past

00:55:30.179 --> 00:55:32.339
we're in an age now
where we have global camaraderie.

00:55:32.339 --> 00:55:33.525
We have friends around the world.

00:55:33.525 --> 00:55:36.215
We have technology
to make good decisions, lots of resources.

00:55:36.215 --> 00:55:37.095
-Sure.

00:55:37.095 --> 00:55:39.811
-Suddenly we could start
operating much more efficiently.

00:55:39.811 --> 00:55:42.386
Starting to see our neighbors
more as friends.

00:55:42.386 --> 00:55:44.066
Caring about strangers.

00:55:44.523 --> 00:55:47.019
That, according to capitalism
is kind of crazy.

00:55:47.019 --> 00:55:48.922
Where we're taught
that every person is a competitor

00:55:48.922 --> 00:55:50.350
in a battle for scarce goods

00:55:50.350 --> 00:55:52.470
but people don't actually have
infinite wants and needs.

00:55:52.470 --> 00:55:54.468
They're willing to sacrifice
for other people

00:55:54.468 --> 00:55:59.169
and in a place like Ukraine
where capitalism isn't quite as pervasive

00:55:59.169 --> 00:56:02.628
I think that's what leads to a lot more
of the well-rounded curiosity about life.

00:56:02.628 --> 00:56:04.507
Not counting minutes as much.

00:56:04.507 --> 00:56:07.328
About people just being more people
to each other on some levels.

00:56:07.328 --> 00:56:10.962
Being a lot more real, not putting on
fake faces for first impressions

00:56:10.962 --> 00:56:13.415
or to be liked, or to climb socially.

00:56:13.415 --> 00:56:17.146
You get a lot of the hoarding mentality
unfortunately as well from scarcity

00:56:17.146 --> 00:56:18.598
but because the capitalism...
-Here?

00:56:18.598 --> 00:56:22.142
-Yeah, especially the oligarchs
who hoarded money, they sit on it.

00:56:22.142 --> 00:56:25.428
They're not really investing it
back into the communities a whole lot.

00:56:25.428 --> 00:56:30.093
But for the most part
I think that the people here

00:56:30.093 --> 00:56:32.155
while not being as good
at marketing and business

00:56:32.155 --> 00:56:34.303
because of less-developed capitalism

00:56:34.303 --> 00:56:36.585
are actually a little bit happier
in a lot of ways

00:56:36.585 --> 00:56:41.385
because of the more relaxed connection
to life that they have because of it.

00:56:41.953 --> 00:56:44.076
-I totally... I totally agree.

00:56:44.076 --> 00:56:45.076
That's...

00:56:45.610 --> 00:56:47.410
I can't explain that to people here.

00:56:47.410 --> 00:56:49.496
You just said it perfectly

00:56:49.496 --> 00:56:52.260
but when I say it's less...

00:56:52.260 --> 00:56:55.452
just manic and anxious ridden.

00:56:56.109 --> 00:56:57.277
Nobody believes me

00:56:57.277 --> 00:57:00.351
and the anxiety, I think,
comes in a different way.

00:57:00.351 --> 00:57:02.346
There are more stresses for basic needs.

00:57:02.346 --> 00:57:04.698
I think for a lot of the population

00:57:04.698 --> 00:57:10.165
but there still doesn't seem to be
the manic battle for time

00:57:10.165 --> 00:57:14.389
and one place to look at,
like, if you take a snapshot of a society.

00:57:15.457 --> 00:57:17.137
Look at parks, right?

00:57:17.777 --> 00:57:22.500
Are there people, normal people,
hanging out in the parks, right?

00:57:22.915 --> 00:57:25.044
Okay, we can say
San Francisco's got great parks

00:57:25.044 --> 00:57:27.641
and there's society
hanging out in the parks

00:57:27.641 --> 00:57:30.706
but I'm gonna say
a snapshot of the US in general

00:57:30.706 --> 00:57:33.586
parks quite often have a lot of bums...

00:57:33.586 --> 00:57:34.472
[scoffs]

00:57:34.472 --> 00:57:35.672
A lot of feces.

00:57:36.694 --> 00:57:40.729
It's not a place for,
say, normal or educated...

00:57:41.423 --> 00:57:43.840
Say, successful people to hang out in.

00:57:43.840 --> 00:57:46.474
And when you walk in the parks here

00:57:46.474 --> 00:57:48.154
they're super uplifting.

00:57:48.916 --> 00:57:53.798
For the most part, uplifting, positive,
and very human experiences, right?

00:57:53.798 --> 00:57:58.136
Like all the guys playing chess
at this Shevchenko park, right?

00:57:58.136 --> 00:58:00.808
Like, all these old guys
come out and play chess

00:58:00.808 --> 00:58:05.019
and I just watch sometimes
and there's community in that

00:58:05.019 --> 00:58:09.678
or people sitting on those chairs
just conversing and taking breaks.

00:58:09.678 --> 00:58:11.411
-There's so much more life.

00:58:11.566 --> 00:58:13.843
-It feels more human, man.

00:58:13.843 --> 00:58:15.672
Where I feel, San Francisco...

00:58:15.672 --> 00:58:17.737
I love it and there's so many great things

00:58:17.737 --> 00:58:19.638
and I'm not saying what's better or worse

00:58:19.638 --> 00:58:21.438
but there's more of that...

00:58:21.795 --> 00:58:23.450
That grind and that pull.

00:58:23.450 --> 00:58:28.461
-Through a TV screen
you can see staged happiness.

00:58:28.461 --> 00:58:31.171
Through economics you can see
people having more stuff

00:58:31.171 --> 00:58:34.531
for working relatively
about the same amount

00:58:34.531 --> 00:58:36.718
but there are those hidden costs.

00:58:36.718 --> 00:58:39.807
-America is very easy to show tangibly.

00:58:39.807 --> 00:58:45.502
Like in a iPhone, in Facebook,
in these movies that people see on TV.

00:58:46.242 --> 00:58:50.660
There's no way to show that feeling
that you just described a little bit ago.

00:58:50.660 --> 00:58:51.660
On the streets...

00:58:51.660 --> 00:58:52.995
There's no way to package that

00:58:52.995 --> 00:58:55.083
and ship that off
to show to another country.

00:58:55.083 --> 00:58:56.890
You have to either come here, feel it,

00:58:56.890 --> 00:59:00.095
or what I've been trying to do
through my content is, like,

00:59:00.095 --> 00:59:04.143
show it through stories or write about it

00:59:04.143 --> 00:59:07.070
and, like, really unearth what that is.

00:59:07.070 --> 00:59:11.608
'Cause again, you can't put it in something
you can touch, taste, and feel.

00:59:11.608 --> 00:59:13.525
-In America we move around a lot.

00:59:13.525 --> 00:59:15.559
We get different jobs.
We're kind of fragmented.

00:59:15.559 --> 00:59:18.459
We lose a lot of our tribes
and even our families.

00:59:18.459 --> 00:59:20.790
I don't think the average American
talks to their families that much

00:59:20.790 --> 00:59:23.990
but in Ukraine I feel like that base...

00:59:23.990 --> 00:59:26.433
That not only gives you
some level of support

00:59:26.433 --> 00:59:29.819
but also a lot of love and general
necessary satisfaction

00:59:29.819 --> 00:59:32.731
for human well-being
is a lot stronger here.

00:59:32.731 --> 00:59:35.957
A lot of Americans, the reason
that they're trying to seem so happy

00:59:35.957 --> 00:59:38.072
and follow all the latest
health trends and everything

00:59:38.072 --> 00:59:40.782
is because they feel sad and alone.

00:59:40.782 --> 00:59:42.503
A lot of that's why I'm starting GameTree

00:59:42.503 --> 00:59:45.795
is because it's so hard to find
that solid group of friends

00:59:45.795 --> 00:59:48.213
if you're moving around a lot
from place to place

00:59:48.213 --> 00:59:50.328
but I've noticed that
people here, on average,

00:59:50.328 --> 00:59:53.076
do have a lot stronger bonds
with their friends and family.

00:59:53.076 --> 00:59:55.021
And that's something
that's just, at the end of the day

00:59:55.021 --> 00:59:59.245
when people are on their death bed
say is the most important thing.

01:00:00.358 --> 01:00:01.431
-It's true.

01:00:01.431 --> 01:00:02.711
Yeah, well said.

01:00:03.569 --> 01:00:06.746
-You can go to a doctor here and...

01:00:07.536 --> 01:00:10.470
It's gonna cost, like, $13.

01:00:10.470 --> 01:00:12.570
-In America it's gonna cost a few hundred.

01:00:12.570 --> 01:00:14.186
-You have to pay a bribe
and he might be drunk.

01:00:14.210 --> 01:00:15.554
This is a possibility of this happening.

01:00:15.554 --> 01:00:18.885
-The healthcare here
is much better despite everything.

01:00:18.885 --> 01:00:20.153
-Dude, how about this...

01:00:20.153 --> 01:00:22.201
The healthcare is better here
if you need little things.

01:00:22.201 --> 01:00:26.473
If you need little things done
and to go to a doctor, it's better here

01:00:26.473 --> 01:00:28.115
and it's way more manageable.

01:00:28.463 --> 01:00:31.583
If I need brain surgery done,
my brain's totally off...

01:00:31.583 --> 01:00:34.747
I wanna go to Stanford
or like the top medical center in the US.

01:00:35.911 --> 01:00:39.142
It's funny you say weed because that...

01:00:39.885 --> 01:00:45.633
One of the observations I noticed early on
was weed is considered a hard drug here.

01:00:45.633 --> 01:00:47.602
Like, drugs are put in one category.

01:00:47.602 --> 01:00:51.425
Like, heroin, cocaine, weed,
are in one basket, right?

01:00:51.425 --> 01:00:54.378
And where we're from it's legal right now.

01:00:54.378 --> 01:00:59.314
You walk into a store just like
you're gonna buy a bottle of alcohol.

01:00:59.314 --> 01:01:01.099
You walk in, you choose what you want.

01:01:01.099 --> 01:01:04.346
You pay the cashier,
you get a bag, and you leave, right?

01:01:04.346 --> 01:01:06.946
-Here it's...
-It's almost like we've...

01:01:07.505 --> 01:01:08.805
PETER: It's all perspective, right?

01:01:08.805 --> 01:01:10.533
And it's all how we've been conditioned.

01:01:10.533 --> 01:01:13.511
So now we're conditioned in California

01:01:13.511 --> 01:01:15.481
or a handful or other states in the US

01:01:15.481 --> 01:01:19.848
where it's completely okay
to purchase in the open.

01:01:19.848 --> 01:01:20.937
It's accepted.

01:01:20.937 --> 01:01:23.086
Here it's not accepted at all I found

01:01:23.086 --> 01:01:26.807
and I smoke weed probably
five, six times a year.

01:01:26.807 --> 01:01:28.694
I don't even drink anymore.

01:01:28.694 --> 01:01:32.790
But that's, like, my escape,
five, six times, have a little weed...

01:01:33.987 --> 01:01:37.396
JOHN: My personal purpose is to help

01:01:37.396 --> 01:01:39.664
fix some things I see
fundamentally broken in society.

01:01:39.664 --> 01:01:42.336
That are universally broken
in a capitalist system

01:01:42.336 --> 01:01:44.999
but that's what led me here.

01:01:44.999 --> 01:01:46.937
Through GameTree,
the company that I'm starting.

01:01:46.937 --> 01:01:50.913
I see if I play my cards right, having
a route that can help a lot of people.

01:01:50.913 --> 01:01:52.180
What I didn't expect so much

01:01:52.180 --> 01:01:55.132
was to actually fall in love
with the country itself.

01:01:55.132 --> 01:01:56.542
With the people here.

01:01:56.542 --> 01:01:58.700
Finding a home here
and wanting to stay here.

01:01:58.700 --> 01:02:02.505
Like, that wasn't as big a part of
the equation when I originally came here.

01:02:02.505 --> 01:02:05.428
Everybody loves an underdog,
myself being no exception.

01:02:05.428 --> 01:02:07.161
It's like a universal human trait

01:02:07.161 --> 01:02:10.451
and I think that Ukraine
just doesn't get their fair share.

01:02:10.451 --> 01:02:13.277
It has a government
that's somewhat corrupt.

01:02:13.277 --> 01:02:14.951
So other countries
don't want to deal with it.

01:02:14.951 --> 01:02:18.706
It has Russia invading 
so it looks like the place is at war.

01:02:18.706 --> 01:02:21.407
A hot potato that nobody wants to touch.

01:02:21.407 --> 01:02:25.368
But the fact is that
this place is a real hidden gem.

01:02:25.368 --> 01:02:27.274
The streets are clean.

01:02:27.274 --> 01:02:28.713
Society is very civil.

01:02:28.713 --> 01:02:30.449
The people are creative and smart.

01:02:30.449 --> 01:02:32.233
The prices are phenomenal.

01:02:32.233 --> 01:02:36.379
To me, this is literally the best place
in the world to be, on a lot of levels

01:02:36.379 --> 01:02:40.406
and that's not something I totally expected
outside of a business realm.

01:02:40.406 --> 01:02:43.900
And so that's what's really
made this place a home for me

01:02:43.900 --> 01:02:47.069
and somewhere that
I'm actually continuing to stay

01:02:47.069 --> 01:02:49.503
despite the potential to now
maybe go somewhere else

01:02:49.503 --> 01:02:50.746
and just manage remotely.

01:02:50.746 --> 01:02:54.153
When I first came here
I was half thinking I would build a team

01:02:54.153 --> 01:02:55.763
and then go back home
or go somewhere else.

01:02:55.763 --> 01:02:56.479
-Right.

01:02:56.479 --> 01:02:58.142
-But within a few days

01:02:58.142 --> 01:03:01.980
just seeing how raw, and real,
and how much potential is in this place

01:03:01.980 --> 01:03:05.244
and how much beauty there is
that I wasn't expecting

01:03:05.244 --> 01:03:08.703
has made me want to be a Ukrainian myself.

01:03:08.703 --> 01:03:10.453
And it's just another way of, like...

01:03:10.453 --> 01:03:14.037
-Would you... You wouldn't get rid of
your American side?

01:03:15.420 --> 01:03:18.625
-I can't because that's where I'm from

01:03:18.625 --> 01:03:22.085
but when I see somebody
like Donald Trump...

01:03:22.769 --> 01:03:25.346
Trumping around America, it's embarrassing.

01:03:25.346 --> 01:03:27.458
-Embarrassing sh*t but also, like...

01:03:27.458 --> 01:03:29.542
The leadership here
is pretty embarrassing too.

01:03:29.542 --> 01:03:33.382
-That's true but Trump's like...
He's like... [both laughing]

01:03:33.565 --> 01:03:35.499
-He's an absolute catastrophe.

01:03:35.798 --> 01:03:36.798
-Um...

01:03:37.517 --> 01:03:41.939
A lot of people are coming to Ukraine
for cheap prices and women.

01:03:42.418 --> 01:03:43.418
And...

01:03:44.043 --> 01:03:47.742
It kind of reminds me of,
like, a Vietnamese brothel.

01:03:47.742 --> 01:03:49.453
When you put it that way.

01:03:49.453 --> 01:03:52.873
And it's kind of sad for me
because the people here

01:03:52.873 --> 01:03:57.648
have so much creativity
and brightness in them

01:03:57.648 --> 01:04:02.640
and I somehow want people to know
that that's not what it is for me.

01:04:03.236 --> 01:04:07.421
Even though that's such a clear thing
that people know about this place

01:04:07.421 --> 01:04:09.436
but I think that this place
really is special

01:04:09.436 --> 01:04:10.739
and has so much more to offer.

01:04:10.739 --> 01:04:11.580
-Sure.

01:04:11.580 --> 01:04:14.208
I mean I lived in Thailand,
I lived in Bangkok.

01:04:14.208 --> 01:04:16.159
You know the stereotype of Bangkok, right?

01:04:16.159 --> 01:04:16.879
-Yeah.

01:04:16.879 --> 01:04:18.906
-And I loved the city.

01:04:18.906 --> 01:04:22.689
And there is that side of it,
there is that whored-out side to it

01:04:22.689 --> 01:04:27.209
but actually in Thai culture
it was seriously frowned upon.

01:04:27.209 --> 01:04:30.765
Like, it's actually...
Thai culture, in a way, is conservative

01:04:30.765 --> 01:04:35.747
and I lived in a completely different
universe on that front

01:04:35.747 --> 01:04:38.309
but I always saw people
were representing...

01:04:38.309 --> 01:04:41.511
I mean people were
referring to the Thais as this

01:04:41.511 --> 01:04:44.227
and how that can really
irritate a population

01:04:44.227 --> 01:04:45.956
and how it misrepresents.

01:04:45.956 --> 01:04:47.953
'Cause there's different sides, right?

01:04:47.953 --> 01:04:50.944
Where the package tours go
in Pattaya is gross.

01:04:50.944 --> 01:04:51.965
It's disgusting.

01:04:51.965 --> 01:04:55.235
Like, you can't even believe
this sh*t happens on planet earth.

01:04:55.235 --> 01:04:59.183
And then there's other segments of
Thai society that are absolutely beautiful

01:04:59.183 --> 01:05:00.198
and apart from that.

01:05:00.198 --> 01:05:03.487
So I don't think Ukraine's
as extreme as that.

01:05:03.487 --> 01:05:06.471
Like, Thailand's an extreme example, right?

01:05:06.471 --> 01:05:11.269
But yeah, here,
there's that extraction mentality.

01:05:11.269 --> 01:05:14.199
I think foreigners come in just to extract

01:05:14.199 --> 01:05:17.491
and there's also foreigners that come in

01:05:17.491 --> 01:05:21.523
that actually truly like and respect
the culture for what it is.

01:05:22.041 --> 01:05:24.034
-For me, being polyamorous

01:05:24.034 --> 01:05:27.149
this is a way worse place to be
than San Francisco.

01:05:27.149 --> 01:05:29.580
Even though the women
are known for being beautiful here

01:05:29.580 --> 01:05:31.892
automatic disqualification
a lot of the time.

01:05:32.178 --> 01:05:33.911
-You're this polyamorous guy.

01:05:33.911 --> 01:05:38.920
Where that doesn't really fly with women...
[chuckles] anywhere in the world.

01:05:38.920 --> 01:05:39.869
Seriously.

01:05:39.869 --> 01:05:42.507
And so you're on the front lines of it
in San Francisco, right?

01:05:42.507 --> 01:05:44.067
And maybe there's...

01:05:44.067 --> 01:05:48.163
I would give less than 1% of females
are gonna be okay with that.

01:05:48.909 --> 01:05:52.769
Then you take it to a
culturally conservative part of the world

01:05:52.769 --> 01:05:54.453
and

01:05:54.453 --> 01:05:57.320
I think you sort of
come up to a steel wall.

01:05:57.850 --> 01:06:01.400
If you're trying to be polyamorous in Kyiv.

01:06:01.912 --> 01:06:04.501
So that, you've put yourself into...

01:06:04.501 --> 01:06:08.341
Well, let's just say a harder climb
than you'd have at home.

01:06:08.435 --> 01:06:11.102
-And despite that, I still love Ukraine.

01:06:13.199 --> 01:06:14.895
-Cool Juke, well...

01:06:14.895 --> 01:06:17.723
Dude, I would like to recap this
in like a year and see where you're at

01:06:17.723 --> 01:06:18.850
'cause I think, like....

01:06:18.850 --> 01:06:22.720
I think when you put yourself
in a completely different environment

01:06:22.720 --> 01:06:25.825
personal growth comes exponentially.

01:06:25.825 --> 01:06:29.532
Like, I feel a year and
a couple months here, that's all it's been.

01:06:29.532 --> 01:06:31.331
I've grown in so many areas.

01:06:31.331 --> 01:06:32.891
I've learned so much.

01:06:32.891 --> 01:06:35.484
I've been challenged
in so many different ways

01:06:35.484 --> 01:06:37.377
but if I was at home

01:06:37.377 --> 01:06:40.434
it would've been, like, this much
growth and learning.

01:06:40.434 --> 01:06:42.367
Where here is, like, this much.

01:06:42.691 --> 01:06:45.224
You've been a good bro in this journey

01:06:45.224 --> 01:06:46.744
and if it wasn't...

01:06:47.588 --> 01:06:49.790
...for you, if we didn't have each other

01:06:49.790 --> 01:06:51.874
neither of us would be here I don't think.

01:06:51.874 --> 01:06:53.154
-In a way.
-Yeah.

01:06:53.565 --> 01:06:56.283
-Not that we need each other to stay here

01:06:56.283 --> 01:06:59.315
but the conversation started
over a breakfast

01:06:59.315 --> 01:07:01.988
and you were thinking of it
and I was supporting it

01:07:01.988 --> 01:07:04.495
and then you planted the seed in my head.

01:07:04.495 --> 01:07:06.425
-And I came and visited.
-After you planted it in mine.

01:07:06.425 --> 01:07:08.186
-Yeah, after... Yeah, exactly.

01:07:08.186 --> 01:07:09.186
So...

01:07:09.210 --> 01:07:10.338
Right on, brother.

01:07:10.338 --> 01:07:14.137
-Kind of like made a nice little comfy
landing pad for you in the apartment.

01:07:14.137 --> 01:07:14.955
-Yeah, you did.

01:07:14.955 --> 01:07:17.147
It was like, what is that weapon?

01:07:17.147 --> 01:07:19.367
-Where it's got the...
-The grappling hook or something?

01:07:19.367 --> 01:07:22.800
-Grappling hook, right, and I just came
over on the line and landed in.

01:07:22.800 --> 01:07:25.600
You did all the hard work, so thanks, man.

